WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.409 align:middle line:84%
[SQUEAKING]
[RUSTLING] [CLICKING]

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:17.570 align:middle line:90%


00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:23.860 align:middle line:84%
GLENN ELLISON: Today I'm going
to do some empirical literature

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:24.360 align:middle line:90%
on entry.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:28.640 align:middle line:84%
And I wanted to start with
this really classic paper

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:31.100 align:middle line:90%
by Bresnahan and Reiss.

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.760 align:middle line:84%
And Bresnahan and Reiss-- it's
actually-- it's an entry paper,

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.500 align:middle line:84%
but it's really a static
competition paper.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.420 align:middle line:84%
So the research question
here is, how empirically

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.700 align:middle line:84%
does level of markups
and competition

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.130 align:middle line:84%
change when you have
more firms in the market?

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:47.930 align:middle line:90%
Is it true that--

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.030 align:middle line:84%
I guess maybe it
is the entry graph.

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.147 align:middle line:84%
What does this graph
look like when--

00:00:52.147 --> 00:00:56.260 align:middle line:90%


00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.460 align:middle line:84%
what does the
price/cost margin look

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:00.680 align:middle line:90%
like as the number of firms--

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:05.060 align:middle line:84%
or their profits look like as
the number of firms enters.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:08.300 align:middle line:84%
And Bresnahan and Reiss are
starting from this classic NEIO

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.260 align:middle line:84%
approach of thinking that it's
very hard to estimate profits

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.140 align:middle line:84%
because it's very hard
to estimate costs.

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.970 align:middle line:84%
In many businesses, there
are lots of intangible costs.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.710 align:middle line:84%
And you can't just
[INAUDIBLE] to get that.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:23.445 align:middle line:84%
So we want to use something
that's readily observable,

00:01:23.445 --> 00:01:26.710 align:middle line:84%
and the idea is that entry
is very clearly observable,

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:31.020 align:middle line:84%
whereas the [INAUDIBLE]
costs observed are not.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.700 align:middle line:84%
The other idea in
Bresnahan and Reiss

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.910 align:middle line:84%
is that in IO, one of
the challenges is always,

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.220 align:middle line:84%
how do you get a cross
enough-- a big enough cross

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:42.810 align:middle line:84%
section of observations to run
the significant statistical

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:43.690 align:middle line:90%
estimates?

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:46.170 align:middle line:84%
And Bresnahan and
Reiss had this idea

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.880 align:middle line:84%
of looking at
isolated small towns.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.715 align:middle line:84%
And so if you look
at the United States,

00:01:50.715 --> 00:01:52.590 align:middle line:84%
one thing that's nice
about the United States

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.340 align:middle line:90%
is it's very, very big.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:55.980 align:middle line:84%
And there are many
parts of it where

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.580 align:middle line:90%
there aren't a lot of people.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:00.720 align:middle line:84%
And so that gives you a lot of
towns where there are towns,

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.980 align:middle line:84%
and then there's very
little around them.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.760 align:middle line:90%
And so if you think about--

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.890 align:middle line:84%
you go search around
the United States,

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:10.720 align:middle line:84%
it's a big enough country that
you can-- if you want towns--

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.360 align:middle line:84%
and in Bresnahan
and Reiss's case,

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.730 align:middle line:90%
what they're looking for is--

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:16.710 align:middle line:90%
I forget what the cut-offs are.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.220 align:middle line:84%
You have towns that
have at least--

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:23.340 align:middle line:84%
you have cities that are
isolated in the sense of--

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.640 align:middle line:84%
I think they're all 100,000
miles from any city of 100,000--

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:30.670 align:middle line:84%
they're 100 miles from any
city of 100,000 people.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:33.840 align:middle line:84%
And they are at least 20 miles
from any city of 1,000 people.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.340 align:middle line:84%
So these are all
towns that are just

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.100 align:middle line:84%
completely isolated from
anything else that you would--

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:40.027 align:middle line:90%
any other population centers.

00:02:40.027 --> 00:02:41.860 align:middle line:84%
And they note that there
are a lot of them--

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:45.690 align:middle line:90%
and, in fact, I think they--

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.300 align:middle line:84%
Peter tells stories of
driving around and wanting

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.210 align:middle line:84%
to verify the data by
just driving around

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.970 align:middle line:84%
to all of these small
towns and then asking them,

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.840 align:middle line:84%
where can I get a
plumber in this town?

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.215 align:middle line:84%
Where can I get--
where can I buy

00:02:57.215 --> 00:02:59.100 align:middle line:90%
tires for my car or whatever?

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.150 align:middle line:90%
And so what they did is--

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.590 align:middle line:84%
most of the data
collection was done

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:02.798 align:middle line:90%
through business directories.

00:03:02.798 --> 00:03:05.040 align:middle line:84%
They basically just
looked at a US map,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:06.972 align:middle line:84%
found places that were
completely isolated.

00:03:06.972 --> 00:03:08.430 align:middle line:84%
And then we used
to have this thing

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.400 align:middle line:84%
called yellow pages, which was
like a phone directory, where

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.960 align:middle line:84%
you would look up phone
numbers in a book.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.640 align:middle line:84%
And so people would use these
books to look up phone numbers.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.650 align:middle line:84%
They would find out who was
advertising as a plumber in each

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:20.490 align:middle line:90%
of these towns.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.560 align:middle line:84%
They also use just
business directories.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:26.280 align:middle line:84%
And then they
visited them to see,

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.680 align:middle line:84%
was this data being
accurately collected?

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.392 align:middle line:90%


00:03:31.392 --> 00:03:34.920 align:middle line:84%
The paper-- it's a
single cross-section.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.820 align:middle line:84%
They don't observe firms
entering in or exiting.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.200 align:middle line:84%
But what they're going to
infer is that sometime prior

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.480 align:middle line:84%
to the point at which
they're collecting the data,

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.710 align:middle line:90%
this firm's--

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:51.570 align:middle line:84%
classic game is
firms choose in/out.

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:57.645 align:middle line:84%
And then firms choose prices
or quantity star of n--

00:03:57.645 --> 00:03:59.170 align:middle line:90%
q star of n.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:02.930 align:middle line:90%


00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.240 align:middle line:90%
They paid a fixed cost.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:05.990 align:middle line:84%
They assume that this
game has been played

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:07.080 align:middle line:90%
at some point in the past.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.290 align:middle line:84%
And so what we're observing
in each market is,

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:12.520 align:middle line:84%
what's the equilibrium number
of firms in that market?

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.640 align:middle line:90%


00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.920 align:middle line:84%
The basic thing the
paper then tries to do

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:21.440 align:middle line:84%
is say, if we can observe
the number of firms

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.870 align:middle line:84%
in a large cross-section
of markets, that's

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:25.940 align:middle line:84%
going to give us a
means to estimate profit

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:31.980 align:middle line:84%
levels without any data on
prices or on costs or on demand.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:35.133 align:middle line:90%
And so consider of basic model.

00:04:35.133 --> 00:04:36.300 align:middle line:90%
You have the entry decision.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:37.860 align:middle line:84%
You have a firm that
has fixed costs,

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:41.170 align:middle line:84%
F. There's going to be
symmetric competition.

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:45.060 align:middle line:84%
So if there are N firms
in a market with S people,

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.510 align:middle line:84%
you're going to get S over N--
so the total number of customers

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:53.700 align:middle line:84%
divided by the number of firms
times pN minus c D of pN.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.730 align:middle line:84%
And so they're going to call
that's S over N times pi N.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:02.040 align:middle line:84%
So pi N is the per
population profit--

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.910 align:middle line:84%
or per population served
profit in this market.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:09.227 align:middle line:84%
It reflects both sales you
make to each customer-- there's

00:05:09.227 --> 00:05:11.310 align:middle line:84%
obviously only some fraction
of the consumers that

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:12.160 align:middle line:90%
are buying from you.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.060 align:middle line:84%
And so this is profit
per person that's

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:16.690 align:middle line:90%
in your part of the market.

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:20.430 align:middle line:84%
So if you have a town, and
roughly, it's divided like this,

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:23.140 align:middle line:84%
and each firm gets one
quarter of the market,

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:27.300 align:middle line:84%
it's your per customer profit
for the market that you serve.

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.500 align:middle line:84%
We're going to have N
firms in equilibrium

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:33.070 align:middle line:84%
if S over N times pi N is
bigger than the fixed cost.

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:36.010 align:middle line:84%
But S over N plus
1 times pi N plus 1

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:37.570 align:middle line:84%
would be less than
the fixed cost.

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:39.120 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, this
is decreasing--

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.340 align:middle line:84%
this is smaller than
this, both because you're

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:45.520 align:middle line:84%
dividing the city
into more fractions,

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:47.680 align:middle line:84%
and the per customer
profit goes down

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.670 align:middle line:90%
because the price goes down.

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:53.100 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going to
say is what asset would

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.790 align:middle line:90%
be the minimum size in which--

00:05:56.790 --> 00:06:00.810 align:middle line:84%
so, for instance, let's
put N on this axis,

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:03.120 align:middle line:84%
and then you can put
the number of doctors

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:05.485 align:middle line:90%
in a town on this axis.

00:06:05.485 --> 00:06:07.860 align:middle line:84%
And what you would observe,
if you could draw this graph,

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:12.600 align:middle line:84%
is towns that have no-- that are
very small don't have a doctor.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:14.280 align:middle line:84%
And then there's
some threshold--

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.230 align:middle line:90%
maybe it's like 800 people--

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:19.830 align:middle line:84%
where now there's a
doctor in the town.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:27.490 align:middle line:84%
And then some other threshold
where 2,000 people were.

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:30.255 align:middle line:84%
Now you have to have
two doctors, and then

00:06:30.255 --> 00:06:32.713 align:middle line:84%
some other threshold where you
tend to have three doctors--

00:06:32.713 --> 00:06:36.690 align:middle line:90%


00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:39.500 align:middle line:90%
2, 3.

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:43.010 align:middle line:84%
And so the idea is that we
can estimate these cut-off--

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:44.370 align:middle line:90%
we can estimate these curves.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:47.640 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, in practice, you're
going to see a lot of zeros.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:51.230 align:middle line:84%
You may see some doctors
even in very small towns.

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:53.660 align:middle line:84%
You'll still see some
towns without doctors here,

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:58.190 align:middle line:84%
but you'll see a lot of 1s, and
maybe you'll even see some 2s.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:01.010 align:middle line:84%
But there's going to be, in
general, some curve like this

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:03.860 align:middle line:84%
where this is--
typically, you need

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:09.410 align:middle line:84%
to have S1 customers
to have one doctor.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:11.730 align:middle line:90%
You need S2 to have two doctors.

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:16.730 align:middle line:84%
Now, here, you need S3
to have three doctors.

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:18.590 align:middle line:84%
And so what they want
to do is estimate

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:20.030 align:middle line:90%
what does this look like?

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:23.060 align:middle line:84%
How many people do you need to
have before the first doctor

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:23.992 align:middle line:90%
comes in?

00:07:23.992 --> 00:07:26.450 align:middle line:84%
How many do you need to have
before the second doctor comes

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:26.810 align:middle line:90%
in?

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:29.227 align:middle line:84%
How many do you need to have
before the third doctor comes

00:07:29.227 --> 00:07:31.500 align:middle line:90%
in?

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.597 align:middle line:84%
Possibly you can estimate this
just by plotting the cities

00:07:34.597 --> 00:07:36.930 align:middle line:84%
with the population on the
x-axis, the number of doctors

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:38.460 align:middle line:90%
on the y-axis.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:40.680 align:middle line:84%
Fit a curve that looks
like this through them--

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.200 align:middle line:90%
see where the jump points are.

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:45.210 align:middle line:90%


00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:49.470 align:middle line:84%
So if you assume that the
fixed costs don't vary with N,

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:55.680 align:middle line:84%
then SN over N is going to be pi
N times pi N is going to be F.

00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.630 align:middle line:84%
And also, SN plus 1 over N plus
1, pi N plus 1 is going to be F.

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:03.420 align:middle line:90%
So both at this point--

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:05.273 align:middle line:84%
at this point you
know the second doctor

00:08:05.273 --> 00:08:07.440 align:middle line:84%
is just indifferent to
entering or not because if it

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:08.732 align:middle line:90%
was smaller, he wouldn't enter.

00:08:08.732 --> 00:08:10.530 align:middle line:84%
If it was bigger,
he would enter.

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:15.670 align:middle line:84%
Here, where the third doctor is
in different entering or not.

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:19.800 align:middle line:84%
So both of these, the profit
that you get if you do come in,

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:22.650 align:middle line:90%
is exactly F.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:26.490 align:middle line:84%
And so if you just set
this equal to this,

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:28.440 align:middle line:90%
and then you divide the--

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.280 align:middle line:84%
bring the pi N down
here in the denominator,

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:33.820 align:middle line:84%
you get that the profit--
with N plus 1 firms divided

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:36.190 align:middle line:84%
by the profit of N
firms is N plus 1

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:39.880 align:middle line:90%
over N, SN over SN plus 1.

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:47.050 align:middle line:84%
You could also write that
as pi N plus 1 over pi N

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:54.840 align:middle line:84%
equals SN over N divided
by SN plus 1 over N plus 1.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:59.250 align:middle line:90%


00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:04.740 align:middle line:84%
So the idea here is that
imagine it takes 1,000 people

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:14.910 align:middle line:84%
in a market to have one
doctor, and S2 is 4,000.

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:16.050 align:middle line:90%
Then this is--

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:18.310 align:middle line:90%
S1 over 1 is 1,000.

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:21.300 align:middle line:84%
You need 1,000 patients in
order to be worthwhile to open

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:22.740 align:middle line:90%
a doctor's office.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:26.430 align:middle line:84%
But then here, S2
over 2 is 2,000.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:28.893 align:middle line:84%
So that would say you need
2,000 people to make it work--

00:09:28.893 --> 00:09:31.560 align:middle line:84%
you need 2,000 patients for the
new doctor to make it worthwhile

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:33.600 align:middle line:84%
to have a second
doctor's office.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:38.400 align:middle line:84%
And from that, we infer that it
must be that pi 2 is just 1/2

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:40.350 align:middle line:90%
of pi 1.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:44.760 align:middle line:84%
So this ratio of this to this
is how much profits per person

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.923 align:middle line:84%
must have declined for the
second doctor to not come in

00:09:47.923 --> 00:09:50.340 align:middle line:84%
until the second doctor is
going to get many more patients

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:51.640 align:middle line:90%
than the first doctor got.

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:55.190 align:middle line:90%


00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:57.830 align:middle line:84%
And so anyway-- so that's
the really powerful idea

00:09:57.830 --> 00:09:58.980 align:middle line:90%
of this paper.

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:02.690 align:middle line:84%
We don't have to have any data
at all on prices or quantities.

00:10:02.690 --> 00:10:07.580 align:middle line:84%
And yet, we can tell
how the ratio of profits

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:10.640 align:middle line:84%
declines as more firms enter
the market by just looking

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:13.310 align:middle line:84%
at how many more customers have
to be there for the second firm

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:14.430 align:middle line:90%
to want to come in.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:17.130 align:middle line:90%


00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:20.460 align:middle line:90%
Question about that basic idea?

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:21.720 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:24.750 align:middle line:84%
The previous slide suggested
what Bresnahan and Reiss

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:29.700 align:middle line:84%
were going to do is put
population on the x-axis, number

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:31.320 align:middle line:84%
of doctors on the
y-axis, and then

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:35.160 align:middle line:84%
just draw a curve like this
that best fit the data.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:38.700 align:middle line:84%
That's not exactly what
they do because they

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.730 align:middle line:84%
want to have a lot of covariates
in the regression that are also

00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:43.270 align:middle line:90%
affecting all of the elements.

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:46.200 align:middle line:84%
So basically, they
set up the models.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:50.370 align:middle line:84%
First, they have something they
call the effect of population.

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:56.550 align:middle line:84%
The effect of population in
city I is its actual population

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:59.330 align:middle line:90%
plus some X--

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:03.810 align:middle line:84%
some explanatory variables, X,
times some coefficients, gamma.

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:06.570 align:middle line:84%
Those X's, they use for things
like the rural population

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:09.030 align:middle line:84%
within 5 miles of the
city, the rural population

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:12.875 align:middle line:84%
within 10 miles of the city, the
growth rate of the population.

00:11:12.875 --> 00:11:14.250 align:middle line:84%
I forget what else
goes in there.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:16.860 align:middle line:84%
But they have the actual
population of the city

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:21.000 align:middle line:84%
plus some controls
for nearby population.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:24.240 align:middle line:84%
Per consumer profits--
again, the idea

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:26.670 align:middle line:84%
is that you would have per
consumer profits, alpha

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:28.020 align:middle line:90%
1 under a monopoly.

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:33.340 align:middle line:84%
Profits alpha 1
under the monopoly.

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:37.620 align:middle line:84%
Alpha 2 would be
duopoly profits.

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:40.405 align:middle line:90%
Alpha 3 would be 3 firms.

00:11:40.405 --> 00:11:42.780 align:middle line:84%
But then you're going to have
other variables that affect

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:44.320 align:middle line:90%
the profitability of a market.

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:48.960 align:middle line:84%
Those variables can include the
per capita income in the city,

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:50.040 align:middle line:90%
the age distribution.

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:53.100 align:middle line:84%
Maybe higher income cities you
earn more profits per customer

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:56.340 align:middle line:90%
or things like that.

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:59.623 align:middle line:84%
The fixed costs-- again,
it's mostly a fixed cost.

00:11:59.623 --> 00:12:02.040 align:middle line:84%
They do allow there to be
covariates that affect the fixed

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:03.310 align:middle line:90%
costs across cities.

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:04.920 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance,
cities that have

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:06.870 align:middle line:84%
more valuable
agricultural land, it

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:11.220 align:middle line:84%
may be more expensive to open up
a tire dealership than in places

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:12.660 align:middle line:90%
with cheaper agricultural land.

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:15.930 align:middle line:84%
Because the land that you need
to buy to set up your tire

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:16.920 align:middle line:90%
shop costs more.

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:22.210 align:middle line:84%
So they have a fixed cost to
also vary with some covariates.

00:12:22.210 --> 00:12:24.000 align:middle line:84%
And then the basic
thought is that-- so I

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:25.000 align:middle line:90%
have all these elements.

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:30.700 align:middle line:84%
I've redefined Si, pi iN, and
Fi to be their covariates.

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:35.340 align:middle line:84%
And then my model is if there
were N firms in the market,

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:41.730 align:middle line:84%
the profits would be Si over N,
pi iN minus Fi plus epsilon i,

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:44.520 align:middle line:84%
where epsilon i is just
a normally distributed

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:47.040 align:middle line:90%
random variable.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:50.730 align:middle line:84%
And so in this model, when are
we going to observe in firms?

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:56.640 align:middle line:84%
We're going to observe
in firms if Si N--

00:12:56.640 --> 00:13:06.060 align:middle line:84%
sorry-- Si over N minus Fi plus
epsilon i is greater than 0.

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:15.000 align:middle line:84%
And Si over n plus 1, pi iN plus
1 minus Fi plus epsilon i equals

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:17.940 align:middle line:90%
0.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:20.100 align:middle line:84%
So we have these
two things that have

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:24.990 align:middle line:84%
to both be true for there to be
exactly N firms in the market.

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:26.700 align:middle line:84%
But you could
write the first one

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:28.680 align:middle line:84%
as epsilon is bigger
than something,

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:31.860 align:middle line:84%
and the second one is epsilon
is less than something.

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:35.280 align:middle line:84%
So if you view epsilon as
a random variable here,

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:38.580 align:middle line:84%
a random shock to
fixed costs, we're

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:40.590 align:middle line:84%
going to observe
N firms whenever

00:13:40.590 --> 00:13:42.150 align:middle line:90%
epsilon is in this interval.

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:44.310 align:middle line:90%
It's between this and this.

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:49.950 align:middle line:84%
And so given the param-- given
all the data and the parameters,

00:13:49.950 --> 00:13:52.110 align:middle line:84%
you can compute the
likelihood of this

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:55.350 align:middle line:84%
as just the normal CDF
evaluated at this point

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:58.360 align:middle line:84%
minus the normal CDF
evaluated at this point.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:01.290 align:middle line:84%
And so then you can estimate
all the parameters--

00:14:01.290 --> 00:14:07.270 align:middle line:84%
gamma, beta, alpha,
delta, gamma--

00:14:07.270 --> 00:14:09.450 align:middle line:90%
this one was lambda.

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:13.560 align:middle line:84%
You can estimate all of
those by maximum likelihood.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:17.700 align:middle line:84%
And that's the basic estimation
technique in the paper.

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:21.150 align:middle line:84%
People-- I know we still
teach ordered probit models

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:21.912 align:middle line:90%
in econometrics.

00:14:21.912 --> 00:14:24.370 align:middle line:84%
This is what people refer to
as ordered probit model, where

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:29.980 align:middle line:84%
you have discrete data
produced by the discretization

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:31.910 align:middle line:84%
of something that has
a normal error in it.

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:35.580 align:middle line:90%


00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:39.150 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, the main things
of interest here coming out

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:42.420 align:middle line:90%
of this estimation are alpha 1--

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:43.990 align:middle line:84%
these were the
objects of interest.

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:46.866 align:middle line:84%
What is the per customer
profits under monopoly?

00:14:46.866 --> 00:14:51.990 align:middle line:90%


00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:53.400 align:middle line:84%
What are the per
customer profits

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:55.470 align:middle line:84%
under monopoly,
duopoly, whatever?

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:57.780 align:middle line:84%
But somehow in the
paper, they really

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:00.340 align:middle line:84%
liked the idea of the
S's as the thresholds.

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:02.970 align:middle line:84%
And so they basically
estimate these things.

00:15:02.970 --> 00:15:04.650 align:middle line:84%
And then they convert
from all the--

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:06.210 align:middle line:84%
they convert from
all the parameters

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:10.260 align:middle line:84%
back to what is the mean
S1, mean S2, mean S3,

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:12.870 align:middle line:84%
holding all the covariates
at their sample means.

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:16.680 align:middle line:84%
And then they do a lot of the
presentation just talking about

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:17.585 align:middle line:90%
what are the S's?

00:15:17.585 --> 00:15:21.320 align:middle line:90%


00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:21.820 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:24.340 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so data
samples-- as I said,

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:26.050 align:middle line:84%
they've got data
on a whole bunch

00:15:26.050 --> 00:15:31.370 align:middle line:84%
of businesses gathered from
202 small towns in Western US.

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:32.630 align:middle line:90%
These are small places.

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:36.310 align:middle line:90%
The mean population is 3,740.

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:39.190 align:middle line:84%
And, as I said, they're all at
least 20 miles away from any

00:15:39.190 --> 00:15:44.214 align:middle line:84%
place that has
1,000 people in it.

00:15:44.214 --> 00:15:47.300 align:middle line:84%
I went to college with
a guy from Wyoming

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:51.372 align:middle line:84%
who said he was 90 miles from
the nearest movie theater.

00:15:51.372 --> 00:15:53.830 align:middle line:84%
So there are parts of the United
States that are like that.

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:57.290 align:middle line:90%


00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:04.430 align:middle line:84%
Here is the data sample showing
the full set of occupation--

00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:06.350 align:middle line:84%
or businesses they started
with and the number

00:16:06.350 --> 00:16:08.240 align:middle line:90%
of firms they get.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:11.580 align:middle line:84%
They end up focusing on the ones
on the top part of the table.

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:12.650 align:middle line:90%
There are some paper--

00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:14.270 align:middle line:84%
some results in
papers about things

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:15.720 align:middle line:90%
farther down in the paper.

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:17.420 align:middle line:90%
But what they wanted--

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:19.130 align:middle line:84%
in some sense, if
you want to talk

00:16:19.130 --> 00:16:20.780 align:middle line:84%
about how the
competition changes

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:22.780 align:middle line:84%
with the number
of firms, you want

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:24.530 align:middle line:84%
there to be a significant
number of cities

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:29.190 align:middle line:84%
that have zero firms,
1, 2, 3, 4, or whatever.

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:33.500 align:middle line:84%
And a lot of these businesses,
it turns out, you never get--

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:36.380 align:middle line:84%
a lot of these
businesses are apparently

00:16:36.380 --> 00:16:39.440 align:middle line:84%
too big businesses to have
many of them in the towns

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:40.940 align:middle line:90%
that they're working with.

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:42.730 align:middle line:84%
So they don't get much
beyond N equals 2.

00:16:42.730 --> 00:16:44.640 align:middle line:84%
But if you look
at the druggist--

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:47.790 align:middle line:90%
I assume this means pharmacies--

00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:51.100 align:middle line:84%
they're very relatively
rare to have no pharmacy.

00:16:51.100 --> 00:16:55.980 align:middle line:84%
But then you do get 1, 2, 3,
and some 4s and 5s and beyond.

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:58.860 align:middle line:84%
Same things with the doctors,
dentists, plumbers, tire

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:00.135 align:middle line:90%
dealers is--

00:17:00.135 --> 00:17:01.740 align:middle line:84%
in some sense,
they focus on these

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:04.260 align:middle line:84%
because they all have at least
some number of markets with 3

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:08.180 align:middle line:84%
and some number
of markets with 4.

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:11.839 align:middle line:84%
There was also a question of how
well-defined are these markets?

00:17:11.839 --> 00:17:17.450 align:middle line:84%
Like, the sum of these things,
like the barbers or whatever--

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:19.635 align:middle line:84%
people who are not barbers
can cut people's hair.

00:17:19.635 --> 00:17:21.260 align:middle line:84%
And so they're sort
of-- it gets harder

00:17:21.260 --> 00:17:24.619 align:middle line:84%
to count how many barbers or
beauticians there are in a town

00:17:24.619 --> 00:17:26.990 align:middle line:84%
because it's just somebody
who works somewhere else--

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:27.795 align:middle line:90%
also cuts hair.

00:17:27.795 --> 00:17:29.920 align:middle line:84%
People know they can go to
that person or whatever.

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:31.500 align:middle line:90%
So it's hard to--

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:34.700 align:middle line:84%
whereas, things like
dentists, I guess,

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:37.480 align:middle line:84%
are easier to identify
that someone is a dentist.

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:39.320 align:middle line:84%
You don't get just
guys who come over

00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:41.650 align:middle line:84%
and drill holes in
your teeth on weekends.

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:45.380 align:middle line:90%


00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:47.910 align:middle line:84%
So what do the entry
thresholds look like?

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:53.690 align:middle line:84%
So, again, the numbers I put up
on the board over there are--

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:57.050 align:middle line:84%
most of these businesses tend
to start to exist in towns,

00:17:57.050 --> 00:17:59.510 align:middle line:84%
and they have fewer
than 1,000 people.

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:02.520 align:middle line:84%
So the doctors-- the
cutoff is 880 people.

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:06.020 align:middle line:84%
The dentist-- the
cut-off is 710 people.

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:08.680 align:middle line:90%
The druggist-- it's 530 people.

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:10.853 align:middle line:84%
The tire dealers,
it's 490 people.

00:18:10.853 --> 00:18:12.520 align:middle line:84%
You need to be bigger
to have a plumber.

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:17.850 align:middle line:84%
That's over 1,000--
1,400 people.

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:21.450 align:middle line:84%
But then if you look at-- let's
just look at-- the druggists

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:23.350 align:middle line:84%
is one of the most
stark examples.

00:18:23.350 --> 00:18:26.430 align:middle line:84%
It seems like you need
500 people to have one.

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:28.930 align:middle line:84%
You need 2,000
people to have two.

00:18:28.930 --> 00:18:32.610 align:middle line:84%
So that's when you have 1,000
customers per drugstore.

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:36.180 align:middle line:90%
To have three, you need 5,000.

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:40.080 align:middle line:84%
So that's like 1,600
people per druggist.

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:45.750 align:middle line:84%
And when they put the ratios
over here and make it clear,

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:50.280 align:middle line:84%
the S2 over S1 ratio-- this is
how many customers per firm you

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:51.640 align:middle line:90%
need to have to have--

00:18:51.640 --> 00:19:00.128 align:middle line:84%
so this is-- they use little sn
to be capital SN over N, which

00:19:00.128 --> 00:19:02.670 align:middle line:84%
is always tough for when you're
putting things on slides that

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:03.280 align:middle line:90%
are small.

00:19:03.280 --> 00:19:07.730 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, these are the
ratio of the per customer--

00:19:07.730 --> 00:19:09.655 align:middle line:84%
the per firm number
of customers,

00:19:09.655 --> 00:19:11.030 align:middle line:84%
which you can
think of as this is

00:19:11.030 --> 00:19:14.370 align:middle line:84%
the thing that's supposed to
estimate pi N over pi N plus 1.

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:20.090 align:middle line:84%
So this is supposed
to be pi 1 over pi 2.

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:22.490 align:middle line:84%
And so what this
is saying is that

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:24.830 align:middle line:84%
in many of these
industries, profits

00:19:24.830 --> 00:19:28.400 align:middle line:84%
are dropping almost in half when
you have-- you have this when

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:31.100 align:middle line:84%
you have a second firm, or per
customer profits dropping almost

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:31.600 align:middle line:90%
in half.

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:34.700 align:middle line:90%
It's like profits-- divide--

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:37.080 align:middle line:84%
you divide it by
either 1.9 or by 1.8.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:39.290 align:middle line:84%
So that's like a 45%,
50% drop in profit

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:41.360 align:middle line:90%
when the second firm comes in.

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:43.910 align:middle line:84%
The one exception
appears to be plumbers,

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:46.410 align:middle line:84%
where we don't get the
first plumber for a while.

00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:52.100 align:middle line:84%
And so it seems that the second
plumber is also serving 50.

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:54.080 align:middle line:84%
You get one plumber when
you have 1,400 people

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:56.413 align:middle line:84%
for the plumber to serve, a
second plumber when you have

00:19:56.413 --> 00:19:59.840 align:middle line:90%
1,500.

00:19:59.840 --> 00:20:03.500 align:middle line:84%
If you look at what happens when
you go-- so one firm to two--

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:05.160 align:middle line:90%
it's a big drop in profit.

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:09.870 align:middle line:84%
When you go from two firms to
three, it varies by industry.

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:13.350 align:middle line:84%
But most of them, other
than the plumbers,

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:18.910 align:middle line:84%
we have a 1 point-- a 10% drop,
30% drop, 60% drop or whatever.

00:20:18.910 --> 00:20:21.910 align:middle line:84%
So it, again, is
a-- or 1 over 1.6.

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:28.170 align:middle line:84%
So, again, you get substantial
drops often going from 2 to 3.

00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:32.280 align:middle line:84%
By the time you're out here,
though, the S5 over S4 numbers,

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:34.303 align:middle line:84%
here, it seems
like competition--

00:20:34.303 --> 00:20:35.970 align:middle line:84%
whatever competition
you're going to get

00:20:35.970 --> 00:20:37.980 align:middle line:84%
is all done by the time
you have four firms.

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:40.680 align:middle line:84%
Like four firms are getting
you just as much competition

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:43.740 align:middle line:90%
as five firms is giving you.

00:20:43.740 --> 00:20:49.260 align:middle line:84%
So the conclusion here of
how basics of competition

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:51.870 align:middle line:84%
seems to work is there's a big
difference between monopoly

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:53.220 align:middle line:90%
and two firms.

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:56.850 align:middle line:84%
It seems like prices keep
going down from 2 to 3.

00:20:56.850 --> 00:20:59.760 align:middle line:84%
And then it seems like we
asymptote pretty quickly

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:03.760 align:middle line:84%
to things are not getting
any more competitive.

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:07.530 align:middle line:90%


00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:08.590 align:middle line:90%
Any questions?

00:21:08.590 --> 00:21:13.545 align:middle line:90%


00:21:13.545 --> 00:21:20.420 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I think it's a fantastic
paper in that this idea of entry

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:22.070 align:middle line:84%
and telling us
about profit levels

00:21:22.070 --> 00:21:24.230 align:middle line:84%
and that being an optimality
condition we can use

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:27.020 align:middle line:84%
has just been
tremendously influential.

00:21:27.020 --> 00:21:28.620 align:middle line:84%
As far as the
applied results go,

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:32.240 align:middle line:84%
I would say the biggest
difficulty with this

00:21:32.240 --> 00:21:34.430 align:middle line:84%
about-- with regard to
this as an applied result

00:21:34.430 --> 00:21:37.100 align:middle line:84%
is this model is
interpreting what

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:41.840 align:middle line:84%
we're seeing as the equilibrium
N star of this game.

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:44.090 align:middle line:84%
So the equilibrium
N star of that game

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:46.372 align:middle line:84%
implies in part that
everybody who is there

00:21:46.372 --> 00:21:47.330 align:middle line:90%
is happy they're there.

00:21:47.330 --> 00:21:49.802 align:middle line:84%
And everyone who's not there
is happy they're not there.

00:21:49.802 --> 00:21:51.260 align:middle line:84%
I think the happy
they're not there

00:21:51.260 --> 00:21:54.130 align:middle line:90%
part I think is very clear.

00:21:54.130 --> 00:21:57.770 align:middle line:84%
The question is, what do we make
of all these doctors in towns

00:21:57.770 --> 00:21:59.030 align:middle line:90%
of 800 people?

00:21:59.030 --> 00:22:01.340 align:middle line:84%
Is the doctor in a
town of 800 people

00:22:01.340 --> 00:22:04.070 align:middle line:84%
because the doctor is
happy serving 800 patients?

00:22:04.070 --> 00:22:06.740 align:middle line:84%
Or is the doctor in a town of
800 people because it was a town

00:22:06.740 --> 00:22:11.550 align:middle line:84%
of 1,500 people and a thriving
farming town in the 1950s when

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:12.550 align:middle line:90%
the doctor moved there.

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:14.430 align:middle line:84%
And the doctor is
now 75 years old

00:22:14.430 --> 00:22:17.160 align:middle line:84%
and doesn't want to leave
the patients the doctor has

00:22:17.160 --> 00:22:20.160 align:middle line:84%
had for 40 years, even
though the number of patients

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:24.750 align:middle line:84%
is dwindling relative to what it
was when the doctor got there.

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:28.470 align:middle line:84%
And if you think about it, if
there's this sort of-- you pay

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:32.250 align:middle line:84%
this sunk cost to enter, and you
don't get your sunk cost back

00:22:32.250 --> 00:22:36.133 align:middle line:84%
when you leave, it could
be that people enter--

00:22:36.133 --> 00:22:37.800 align:middle line:84%
the entry-- the things
that we're seeing

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:39.130 align:middle line:90%
are not entry thresholds.

00:22:39.130 --> 00:22:43.350 align:middle line:84%
They're exit-- we're
seeing exit thresholds.

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:48.410 align:middle line:84%
And the exit thresholds might
not be counting all fixed costs.

00:22:48.410 --> 00:22:51.270 align:middle line:90%


00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:55.230 align:middle line:84%
Another potential issue is
just that counting firms

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:59.480 align:middle line:84%
is more difficult in their
application than in others.

00:22:59.480 --> 00:23:01.980 align:middle line:84%
How do you count someone who
does some plumbing on the side,

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:03.563 align:middle line:84%
or someone who does
some hair cutting?

00:23:03.563 --> 00:23:05.790 align:middle line:84%
So I think the ones in
the licensed professions,

00:23:05.790 --> 00:23:09.460 align:middle line:84%
like the dentists and doctors,
are probably better with that.

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:12.120 align:middle line:84%
But the ones that are more
like the businesses we normally

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:16.200 align:middle line:84%
think about price competition
operating in, some of those

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:17.730 align:middle line:84%
have this issue of
it's going to be

00:23:17.730 --> 00:23:21.870 align:middle line:84%
hard to identify businesses, and
ensure that we're [INAUDIBLE].

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:23.700 align:middle line:84%
For instance, the
result on plumbers

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:27.158 align:middle line:84%
could be that you don't
get a lot of market power

00:23:27.158 --> 00:23:29.700 align:middle line:84%
with one plumber because there's
always somebody else in town

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:32.220 align:middle line:84%
who you can call who just
owns wrenches and can

00:23:32.220 --> 00:23:35.037 align:middle line:84%
come over and fix stuff for
you, even if they're not

00:23:35.037 --> 00:23:35.870 align:middle line:90%
officially plumbers.

00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:39.310 align:middle line:90%


00:23:39.310 --> 00:23:40.282 align:middle line:90%
Questions?

00:23:40.282 --> 00:23:43.000 align:middle line:90%


00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:43.940 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:23:43.940 --> 00:23:49.220 align:middle line:84%
So then the next step I want to
go to was Berry and Waldfogel.

00:23:49.220 --> 00:23:51.950 align:middle line:84%
So this is very
much following up

00:23:51.950 --> 00:23:54.825 align:middle line:84%
on of theory of efficiency
of entry I did last class.

00:23:54.825 --> 00:23:57.270 align:middle line:84%
And so, as I said,
there are reasons

00:23:57.270 --> 00:23:59.270 align:middle line:84%
why entry is going to be
higher or lower than we

00:23:59.270 --> 00:24:00.210 align:middle line:90%
would like it to be.

00:24:00.210 --> 00:24:01.627 align:middle line:84%
And Berry and
Waldfogel are trying

00:24:01.627 --> 00:24:03.530 align:middle line:90%
to say, let's pick an industry.

00:24:03.530 --> 00:24:05.940 align:middle line:84%
Let's say in this industry,
how does it work out?

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:08.700 align:middle line:84%
Are there more firms than we
would like or fewer firms?

00:24:08.700 --> 00:24:11.270 align:middle line:84%
How big are the welfare losses
from the number of firms

00:24:11.270 --> 00:24:12.620 align:middle line:90%
that we observe?

00:24:12.620 --> 00:24:18.340 align:middle line:84%
The industry that they
pick is commercial radio.

00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:21.110 align:middle line:84%
And I think the argument
is that there's--

00:24:21.110 --> 00:24:25.350 align:middle line:84%
you need a license to
open a radio station.

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:28.400 align:middle line:84%
There are some places where
you might-- where there's a--

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:30.733 align:middle line:84%
you can't open a radio station
and broadcast on any band

00:24:30.733 --> 00:24:32.650 align:middle line:84%
because you'd be interfering
with the stations

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:33.390 align:middle line:90%
right around you.

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:35.570 align:middle line:84%
But in most cities
in the United States,

00:24:35.570 --> 00:24:37.580 align:middle line:84%
there are bands
that are available.

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:41.010 align:middle line:84%
There's obviously a fixed cost
of applying for the government

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:43.840 align:middle line:84%
license and opening up and just
putting up a transmission tower.

00:24:43.840 --> 00:24:44.610 align:middle line:90%
But that's fine.

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:46.953 align:middle line:90%
That's exactly all the Fs.

00:24:46.953 --> 00:24:48.870 align:middle line:84%
And so this is an example
of an industry where

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:51.363 align:middle line:84%
we think there is a substantial
F. Because to have a radio

00:24:51.363 --> 00:24:52.530 align:middle line:90%
station, you need equipment.

00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:54.210 align:middle line:90%
You need people there.

00:24:54.210 --> 00:24:58.140 align:middle line:84%
But then we can think
about how are the--

00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:00.810 align:middle line:90%
how is the prices affected.

00:25:00.810 --> 00:25:03.473 align:middle line:90%
Now, radio is free.

00:25:03.473 --> 00:25:04.890 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense,
the price you're

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:07.590 align:middle line:84%
used to thinking about
for radio is zero.

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:10.900 align:middle line:90%
But radio is a platform market.

00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:13.480 align:middle line:84%
So there are two types of
customers at radio stations.

00:25:13.480 --> 00:25:15.930 align:middle line:84%
There are listeners who
typically pay zero to listen.

00:25:15.930 --> 00:25:18.150 align:middle line:84%
But then there are
advertisers who pay money

00:25:18.150 --> 00:25:20.070 align:middle line:90%
to put commercials on.

00:25:20.070 --> 00:25:23.610 align:middle line:90%
And so the standard way that--

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:25.560 align:middle line:84%
because the price is
free, the standard way

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:29.580 align:middle line:84%
that the FTC or antitrust
regulators tend to treat radio

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:33.040 align:middle line:84%
is to focus on the business
of producing advertising.

00:25:33.040 --> 00:25:35.250 align:middle line:84%
So they think of as
a radio station is--

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:39.220 align:middle line:84%
they have this technology
where they buy rights to music.

00:25:39.220 --> 00:25:40.200 align:middle line:90%
They play that music.

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:42.840 align:middle line:84%
That music produces
ears of people.

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:45.180 align:middle line:84%
And then they sell
those ears of people

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:47.610 align:middle line:84%
to advertisers who want
to advertise to them.

00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:51.030 align:middle line:84%
So when we're thinking
about price in this market,

00:25:51.030 --> 00:25:54.150 align:middle line:84%
we should be thinking about
price of advertisers--

00:25:54.150 --> 00:25:59.820 align:middle line:84%
price of-- think of the price
as the price of a listener,

00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:02.850 align:middle line:84%
and think of the firms as having
a technology for producing

00:26:02.850 --> 00:26:03.620 align:middle line:90%
listeners.

00:26:03.620 --> 00:26:05.370 align:middle line:84%
So this technology for
producing listeners

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:08.400 align:middle line:84%
involves something about
radio music quality

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:09.930 align:middle line:90%
or content quality.

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:12.090 align:middle line:84%
And then that produces
people that they then

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:13.900 align:middle line:90%
sell to the advertisers.

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:16.230 align:middle line:84%
So the advertisers
are the consumers.

00:26:16.230 --> 00:26:17.720 align:middle line:90%
The people are ignored.

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:21.480 align:middle line:84%
And one thing you might notice,
if that's your view of radio,

00:26:21.480 --> 00:26:22.500 align:middle line:90%
you'd be like, wait.

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:25.230 align:middle line:84%
Wouldn't that mean like whenever
two radio stations are proposing

00:26:25.230 --> 00:26:27.720 align:middle line:84%
merging, what would
be considered bad

00:26:27.720 --> 00:26:29.820 align:middle line:84%
is if by merging
the radio stations

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:32.040 align:middle line:84%
are going to raise the
price of advertising

00:26:32.040 --> 00:26:36.450 align:middle line:84%
and have fewer commercials per
hour and play music instead.

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:39.632 align:middle line:84%
And that actually
tends to be how

00:26:39.632 --> 00:26:41.340 align:middle line:84%
the US antitrust
authorities have largely

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:42.490 align:middle line:90%
conceptualized radio.

00:26:42.490 --> 00:26:43.948 align:middle line:84%
That is, if there's
something going

00:26:43.948 --> 00:26:46.980 align:middle line:84%
on that's going to result in
fewer commercials per hour

00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:49.833 align:middle line:84%
and more music or more news,
then they're skeptical of it.

00:26:49.833 --> 00:26:51.500 align:middle line:84%
If it's something
that's going to result

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:53.040 align:middle line:84%
in even more
commercials per hour,

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:56.310 align:middle line:84%
that's good because that's
stopping the deadweight loss

00:26:56.310 --> 00:26:58.830 align:middle line:90%
on the advertiser's side.

00:26:58.830 --> 00:27:02.800 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, Berry and
Waldfogel mostly adopt that.

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:05.153 align:middle line:84%
They are clearly well
aware that radio is also

00:27:05.153 --> 00:27:07.320 align:middle line:84%
something people listen to,
and they talk about that

00:27:07.320 --> 00:27:09.750 align:middle line:84%
at the end when all
the analysis is done,

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:12.920 align:middle line:84%
thinking about radio stations as
things that produce advertising

00:27:12.920 --> 00:27:14.056 align:middle line:90%
to sell to advertisers.

00:27:14.056 --> 00:27:18.880 align:middle line:90%


00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:21.318 align:middle line:90%
So we've got 135 US markets.

00:27:21.318 --> 00:27:23.860 align:middle line:84%
We're trying to compare with
what would be socially efficient

00:27:23.860 --> 00:27:26.140 align:middle line:84%
and talk about the
welfare losses.

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:29.500 align:middle line:84%
We're going to focus
on the radio stations

00:27:29.500 --> 00:27:34.010 align:middle line:84%
as firms that have
advertisers as customers.

00:27:34.010 --> 00:27:35.740 align:middle line:90%
They're going to assume--

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:38.990 align:middle line:84%
they're starting with the very
first model of entry that I did.

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:41.410 align:middle line:84%
So they're assuming that the
ears that are being produced

00:27:41.410 --> 00:27:43.150 align:middle line:90%
are a homogeneous good.

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:46.150 align:middle line:84%
So every listener is worth
exactly the same amount

00:27:46.150 --> 00:27:48.640 align:middle line:90%
to every advertiser.

00:27:48.640 --> 00:27:55.360 align:middle line:84%
And, in practice, obviously,
there are going to be stations

00:27:55.360 --> 00:27:58.270 align:middle line:84%
playing music from the 1950s,
where they have lots of ads

00:27:58.270 --> 00:28:00.430 align:middle line:90%
for reverse mortgages.

00:28:00.430 --> 00:28:04.930 align:middle line:84%
There are going to
be stations playing

00:28:04.930 --> 00:28:09.970 align:middle line:84%
much more up-to-date music that
are advertising concert tickets.

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:12.970 align:middle line:84%
Somehow they just they just
abstract away from that.

00:28:12.970 --> 00:28:15.070 align:middle line:84%
It's just-- they're just
going to go to listeners

00:28:15.070 --> 00:28:16.220 align:middle line:90%
are a homogeneous effect.

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:18.470 align:middle line:84%
There's just a
downward-sloping demand

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:22.820 align:middle line:84%
curve for the more listeners
you have, the price--

00:28:22.820 --> 00:28:26.210 align:middle line:84%
or the more listeners you--
the more commercial slots

00:28:26.210 --> 00:28:30.410 align:middle line:84%
you have to sell, lowers the
price per commercial slot.

00:28:30.410 --> 00:28:32.470 align:middle line:84%
And that's how that
market is going to work.

00:28:32.470 --> 00:28:35.660 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so the--
what's also good

00:28:35.660 --> 00:28:39.290 align:middle line:84%
here is they have purely fixed
costs and no marginal costs

00:28:39.290 --> 00:28:40.680 align:middle line:90%
of producing listeners.

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:42.470 align:middle line:84%
So you get even simpler
welfare function

00:28:42.470 --> 00:28:43.970 align:middle line:84%
than the one we
talked about before.

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:47.750 align:middle line:84%
So the welfare-- if you
have-- and radio stations,

00:28:47.750 --> 00:28:49.500 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be
the integral from 0.

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:51.708 align:middle line:84%
So L star of N is going to
be the number of listeners

00:28:51.708 --> 00:28:53.810 align:middle line:84%
per station when
you have N stations.

00:28:53.810 --> 00:28:58.590 align:middle line:84%
So it's just the integral from
0 to N L star of N. P of x dx--

00:28:58.590 --> 00:29:01.220 align:middle line:84%
the willingness to pay for the
very first-- for each listener

00:29:01.220 --> 00:29:03.303 align:middle line:84%
integrated out over the
total number of listeners.

00:29:03.303 --> 00:29:06.230 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
advertiser willingness

00:29:06.230 --> 00:29:08.060 align:middle line:90%
to pay for listeners.

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:10.670 align:middle line:84%
And this is the gross
surplus, is just

00:29:10.670 --> 00:29:15.500 align:middle line:84%
the area under that curve,
minus the fixed costs

00:29:15.500 --> 00:29:18.200 align:middle line:84%
that are incurred in
generating all those listeners.

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:19.990 align:middle line:84%
And so the things that
we need to estimate

00:29:19.990 --> 00:29:23.410 align:middle line:84%
are this per firm
listening function L

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:27.790 align:middle line:84%
star of N. We need the
advertiser inverse demand

00:29:27.790 --> 00:29:32.170 align:middle line:84%
function, L of N. And we
need the fixed cost F. So

00:29:32.170 --> 00:29:34.420 align:middle line:84%
to evaluate welfare
in this perspective,

00:29:34.420 --> 00:29:36.405 align:middle line:84%
you just need to estimate
those three objects.

00:29:36.405 --> 00:29:41.283 align:middle line:90%


00:29:41.283 --> 00:29:42.200 align:middle line:90%
How are we doing this?

00:29:42.200 --> 00:29:44.500 align:middle line:84%
So the first [INAUDIBLE]
of listening, this

00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:49.570 align:middle line:84%
is where you get the sense
of how much NEIO people like

00:29:49.570 --> 00:29:52.150 align:middle line:84%
discrete choice models and
discrete-- discrete choice

00:29:52.150 --> 00:29:53.950 align:middle line:84%
models of estimating
things because they

00:29:53.950 --> 00:29:55.610 align:middle line:90%
don't have any price variation.

00:29:55.610 --> 00:29:57.777 align:middle line:84%
They're not actually trying
to estimate the consumer

00:29:57.777 --> 00:29:58.450 align:middle line:90%
preferences.

00:29:58.450 --> 00:30:00.783 align:middle line:84%
Despite not trying to estimate
the consumer preferences,

00:30:00.783 --> 00:30:04.300 align:middle line:84%
they're still using a model with
consumers having discrete choice

00:30:04.300 --> 00:30:07.360 align:middle line:84%
preferences, choosing among
stations or the outside good

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:10.360 align:middle line:84%
to generate their
listening function.

00:30:10.360 --> 00:30:15.910 align:middle line:84%
So this is-- people know
what nested logit models are?

00:30:15.910 --> 00:30:17.470 align:middle line:90%
So, OK.

00:30:17.470 --> 00:30:20.830 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, this is a-- what
we call a nested logit model.

00:30:20.830 --> 00:30:23.650 align:middle line:84%
Do you remember I sort of-- we
went with the standard logit

00:30:23.650 --> 00:30:25.420 align:middle line:84%
model, the utility
the consumer i

00:30:25.420 --> 00:30:29.320 align:middle line:84%
gets from listening to
station j is delta j,

00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:32.710 align:middle line:84%
which is like the quality of
station j plus epsilon ij, which

00:30:32.710 --> 00:30:34.225 align:middle line:90%
is like the logit type.

00:30:34.225 --> 00:30:37.210 align:middle line:90%


00:30:37.210 --> 00:30:42.480 align:middle line:84%
In the nested logic model,
you add a second shock.

00:30:42.480 --> 00:30:45.320 align:middle line:84%
So the epsilon ij
is the shock that's

00:30:45.320 --> 00:30:46.925 align:middle line:90%
independent across stations.

00:30:46.925 --> 00:30:48.300 align:middle line:84%
I like the music
on this station.

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:49.758 align:middle line:84%
I don't like music
on this station.

00:30:49.758 --> 00:30:53.210 align:middle line:84%
That's additive
to my preferences.

00:30:53.210 --> 00:30:56.840 align:middle line:84%
But then there's also
a common shock, vi.

00:30:56.840 --> 00:30:59.840 align:middle line:84%
And the vi is a
random variable that's

00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:01.850 align:middle line:84%
drawn randomly at the
level of the customer,

00:31:01.850 --> 00:31:03.150 align:middle line:90%
not at the station.

00:31:03.150 --> 00:31:06.620 align:middle line:84%
And it is their taste for music
as a whole versus not listening

00:31:06.620 --> 00:31:07.820 align:middle line:90%
to the radio.

00:31:07.820 --> 00:31:09.680 align:middle line:90%
So we have these two shocks--

00:31:09.680 --> 00:31:15.740 align:middle line:84%
one common shock that customer i
gets regardless of which station

00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:16.790 align:middle line:90%
they listen to.

00:31:16.790 --> 00:31:20.270 align:middle line:84%
And then one set of-- and then
a set of idiosyncratic shocks

00:31:20.270 --> 00:31:23.270 align:middle line:84%
that are independent across
stations that you get if

00:31:23.270 --> 00:31:26.780 align:middle line:84%
and only if you listen to
that particular station.

00:31:26.780 --> 00:31:29.120 align:middle line:84%
And the idea here is
by having a sigma that

00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:31.470 align:middle line:90%
can vary between 0 and 1--

00:31:31.470 --> 00:31:36.140 align:middle line:84%
so sigma equals 0 is the
pure logit model, where

00:31:36.140 --> 00:31:39.980 align:middle line:84%
when you add another
station, it draws customers

00:31:39.980 --> 00:31:42.050 align:middle line:84%
equally from all of
the other stations

00:31:42.050 --> 00:31:43.760 align:middle line:90%
and from the nonlisteners.

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:46.310 align:middle line:84%
And in radio, nonlisteners
are most people.

00:31:46.310 --> 00:31:49.550 align:middle line:84%
In most half hours, most
people in the United States

00:31:49.550 --> 00:31:51.118 align:middle line:90%
are not listening to the radio.

00:31:51.118 --> 00:31:52.410 align:middle line:90%
It's actually surprisingly few.

00:31:52.410 --> 00:31:54.710 align:middle line:84%
It's about-- I
think it's like 85%.

00:31:54.710 --> 00:31:58.340 align:middle line:84%
So typical half hour, 85%
of people in United States

00:31:58.340 --> 00:32:00.290 align:middle line:90%
are not listening to the radio.

00:32:00.290 --> 00:32:03.230 align:middle line:84%
But so then if you have
the pure logit, that

00:32:03.230 --> 00:32:06.740 align:middle line:84%
means when a new station
attracts someone,

00:32:06.740 --> 00:32:08.900 align:middle line:84%
it's 85% chance that
they're expanding the market

00:32:08.900 --> 00:32:11.150 align:middle line:84%
and choosing someone who
wasn't listening to the radio

00:32:11.150 --> 00:32:12.470 align:middle line:90%
to begin with.

00:32:12.470 --> 00:32:17.130 align:middle line:84%
The other extreme, sigma
equals 1, this shock goes away.

00:32:17.130 --> 00:32:19.400 align:middle line:90%
It's all the common shock.

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:21.650 align:middle line:84%
And when it's all
the common shock,

00:32:21.650 --> 00:32:24.170 align:middle line:90%
then it's 100% business dealing.

00:32:24.170 --> 00:32:26.810 align:middle line:84%
If you only have common shocks,
and the idiosyncratic means are

00:32:26.810 --> 00:32:29.300 align:middle line:84%
essentially zero, you
add a new station,

00:32:29.300 --> 00:32:31.800 align:middle line:84%
it's just going to pull people
away from the other stations.

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:33.175 align:middle line:84%
And it's only the
people who have

00:32:33.175 --> 00:32:36.410 align:middle line:84%
the vi's that are big enough to
overcome delta that will listen

00:32:36.410 --> 00:32:40.470 align:middle line:90%
to radio regardless.

00:32:40.470 --> 00:32:43.042 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so this--
they have this model.

00:32:43.042 --> 00:32:44.500 align:middle line:84%
It's got this free
parameter sigma.

00:32:44.500 --> 00:32:46.290 align:middle line:84%
And that's going to let them
say how important business

00:32:46.290 --> 00:32:46.790 align:middle line:90%
dealing is.

00:32:46.790 --> 00:32:48.540 align:middle line:84%
And it's very
important to have that

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:49.923 align:middle line:90%
because a business dealing--

00:32:49.923 --> 00:32:51.840 align:middle line:84%
we know that there's a
lot of business dealing

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:52.993 align:middle line:90%
if there's too much entry.

00:32:52.993 --> 00:32:54.660 align:middle line:84%
If there's very little
business dealing,

00:32:54.660 --> 00:32:59.700 align:middle line:84%
then we might have
insufficient entry.

00:32:59.700 --> 00:33:01.950 align:middle line:90%
If you have a totally--

00:33:01.950 --> 00:33:03.532 align:middle line:84%
if this model was
totally symmetric,

00:33:03.532 --> 00:33:06.240 align:middle line:84%
if all the deltas were the same,
if all the stations were exactly

00:33:06.240 --> 00:33:12.300 align:middle line:84%
the same quality, then the
nested logit model N l star of N

00:33:12.300 --> 00:33:14.790 align:middle line:84%
would be N to the 1
minus sigma power over e

00:33:14.790 --> 00:33:17.500 align:middle line:84%
to the minus delta plus
the N to the 1 minus sigma.

00:33:17.500 --> 00:33:21.150 align:middle line:84%
So again, for sigma equals
1, total listenership

00:33:21.150 --> 00:33:23.640 align:middle line:84%
is just completely
fixed at 1 over 1

00:33:23.640 --> 00:33:25.890 align:middle line:84%
plus e to the minus
delta, regardless

00:33:25.890 --> 00:33:27.430 align:middle line:90%
of the number of consumers.

00:33:27.430 --> 00:33:34.500 align:middle line:84%
But then when sigma goes to
zero, this thing is like--

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:37.980 align:middle line:84%
it's asymptoting towards 1,
but it's going up initially

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:39.830 align:middle line:90%
linearly then.

00:33:39.830 --> 00:33:44.050 align:middle line:84%
Because it's like N to the first
over this plus something small.

00:33:44.050 --> 00:33:45.995 align:middle line:90%
So it's going up again.

00:33:45.995 --> 00:33:53.720 align:middle line:90%


00:33:53.720 --> 00:33:55.670 align:middle line:90%
Anyway, so they estimate this--

00:33:55.670 --> 00:33:58.760 align:middle line:84%
we're estimating this using the
data on how many people listen

00:33:58.760 --> 00:34:01.700 align:middle line:84%
as a function of N. And
they have an IV approach

00:34:01.700 --> 00:34:04.400 align:middle line:90%
where they use population.

00:34:04.400 --> 00:34:07.910 align:middle line:84%
You might overesti--
underestimate the effect of N

00:34:07.910 --> 00:34:10.219 align:middle line:84%
on markets because
you'll see a lot of--

00:34:10.219 --> 00:34:11.900 align:middle line:84%
or overestimate it
because you'd see

00:34:11.900 --> 00:34:14.120 align:middle line:84%
a lot of stations
entering the markets that

00:34:14.120 --> 00:34:15.290 align:middle line:90%
have a lot of demand.

00:34:15.290 --> 00:34:17.623 align:middle line:84%
Like, there are some cities
where people like the radio,

00:34:17.623 --> 00:34:20.540 align:middle line:84%
like Nashville, and some like
Boston where they don't, then

00:34:20.540 --> 00:34:23.270 align:middle line:84%
you might get more firms
in Nashville than in Boston

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:25.190 align:middle line:84%
because they're
meeting the demand.

00:34:25.190 --> 00:34:28.040 align:middle line:84%
And so the instrument
for the number of firms,

00:34:28.040 --> 00:34:30.230 align:middle line:84%
they use the population
as an instrument

00:34:30.230 --> 00:34:31.230 align:middle line:90%
for the number of firms.

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:34.340 align:middle line:84%
And so they're sometimes
looking at how does listening

00:34:34.340 --> 00:34:40.135 align:middle line:84%
compare in cities with a lot
of people versus a few people.

00:34:40.135 --> 00:34:41.760 align:middle line:84%
Then the second thing
they do is just--

00:34:41.760 --> 00:34:44.639 align:middle line:84%
they want to estimate this
downward-sloping advertising

00:34:44.639 --> 00:34:46.770 align:middle line:90%
demand curve.

00:34:46.770 --> 00:34:49.590 align:middle line:84%
They're going to assume that
the advertising prices depend

00:34:49.590 --> 00:34:53.040 align:middle line:84%
on the share of people listening
to the radio and market k.

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:59.160 align:middle line:84%
And so it's just the log of
price of advertising in market k

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:04.660 align:middle line:84%
is equal to some characteristics
of the city, plus something--

00:35:04.660 --> 00:35:07.560 align:middle line:84%
if you have a downward-sloping
demand in the number

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:08.820 align:middle line:90%
of listeners--

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:11.730 align:middle line:84%
listener hours that
there are plus omega k.

00:35:11.730 --> 00:35:15.970 align:middle line:84%
So, again, the idea is the
more listener hours you have,

00:35:15.970 --> 00:35:17.970 align:middle line:84%
the lower is the willingness
to pay per listener

00:35:17.970 --> 00:35:22.800 align:middle line:90%
hour for the commercials.

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:25.410 align:middle line:90%
And then the fixed costs.

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:29.640 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, this is like
Bresnahan and Reiss kind

00:35:29.640 --> 00:35:31.790 align:middle line:90%
of approach, that you just--

00:35:31.790 --> 00:35:34.680 align:middle line:84%
you are trying to rationalize
the number of radio stations

00:35:34.680 --> 00:35:35.490 align:middle line:90%
in every market.

00:35:35.490 --> 00:35:38.220 align:middle line:84%
And so you rationalize
it by assuming

00:35:38.220 --> 00:35:40.830 align:middle line:90%
that there are these errors.

00:35:40.830 --> 00:35:42.420 align:middle line:84%
In this case, they
do errors drawn

00:35:42.420 --> 00:35:44.580 align:middle line:84%
from a log-normal
distribution instead of

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:46.900 align:middle line:90%
from a normal distribution.

00:35:46.900 --> 00:35:49.710 align:middle line:90%
And they do the--

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:51.360 align:middle line:84%
estimate two
parameters-- the mean

00:35:51.360 --> 00:35:55.170 align:middle line:84%
and variance of the
log-normal distribution.

00:35:55.170 --> 00:36:00.150 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, that's just
like Bresnahan and Reiss.

00:36:00.150 --> 00:36:04.720 align:middle line:84%
And the data have information
about 3,285 stations,

00:36:04.720 --> 00:36:06.610 align:middle line:90%
135 markets.

00:36:06.610 --> 00:36:08.640 align:middle line:84%
So they have individual
station characteristics

00:36:08.640 --> 00:36:11.430 align:middle line:84%
that they're using
in some places.

00:36:11.430 --> 00:36:14.430 align:middle line:84%
These regressions, like the
advertising demand regressions,

00:36:14.430 --> 00:36:15.720 align:middle line:90%
those are just run--

00:36:15.720 --> 00:36:18.400 align:middle line:84%
their advertising data is
purely at the market level.

00:36:18.400 --> 00:36:20.940 align:middle line:84%
It's not at the
station market level.

00:36:20.940 --> 00:36:24.710 align:middle line:84%
So there, they're just using
market level advertising prices

00:36:24.710 --> 00:36:27.130 align:middle line:84%
they get from some
consulting firm that

00:36:27.130 --> 00:36:28.940 align:middle line:90%
provides advertising prices.

00:36:28.940 --> 00:36:32.200 align:middle line:84%
I think it actually comes
from the revenues and rate.

00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:35.320 align:middle line:84%
It comes with radio
stations advertising--

00:36:35.320 --> 00:36:38.710 align:middle line:90%
reported advertising revenues.

00:36:38.710 --> 00:36:41.470 align:middle line:84%
The raw data, which
is going to tell you

00:36:41.470 --> 00:36:45.280 align:middle line:84%
a lot about what the results
come out, is here in the paper.

00:36:45.280 --> 00:36:48.130 align:middle line:84%
So this graph is as
the number of stations

00:36:48.130 --> 00:36:51.172 align:middle line:84%
in a market increases,
how does listening go up?

00:36:51.172 --> 00:36:52.630 align:middle line:84%
And you might say,
OK, I definitely

00:36:52.630 --> 00:36:54.400 align:middle line:90%
see that positive slope--

00:36:54.400 --> 00:36:57.310 align:middle line:84%
more radio stations,
more listening.

00:36:57.310 --> 00:37:00.400 align:middle line:84%
But if you look at
this graph, this scale

00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:04.070 align:middle line:84%
is really running from 0.1
to 0.18, or 0.12 to 0.18.

00:37:04.070 --> 00:37:06.540 align:middle line:84%
So when you have
15 radio stations,

00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:09.040 align:middle line:84%
it's looking like you're getting
14% of the people listening

00:37:09.040 --> 00:37:10.810 align:middle line:90%
to the radio.

00:37:10.810 --> 00:37:15.140 align:middle line:84%
When you have 45 radio stations,
you're getting like 15 or 16.

00:37:15.140 --> 00:37:19.060 align:middle line:84%
So we're tripling the number of
radio stations in the market,

00:37:19.060 --> 00:37:23.860 align:middle line:84%
and listening is going from 14%
to 16% or something like that.

00:37:23.860 --> 00:37:28.330 align:middle line:84%
So this is saying that it does
seem like radio station entry is

00:37:28.330 --> 00:37:30.400 align:middle line:90%
mostly business stealing.

00:37:30.400 --> 00:37:33.250 align:middle line:84%
And, again, we think that this
graph, if you drew the OLS,

00:37:33.250 --> 00:37:37.150 align:middle line:84%
might be overstated how much
market expansion there is.

00:37:37.150 --> 00:37:40.060 align:middle line:84%
So when you're on the IV, it's
not surprising that it says,

00:37:40.060 --> 00:37:44.080 align:middle line:84%
yes, there's an awful lot
of business dealing here.

00:37:44.080 --> 00:37:45.430 align:middle line:90%
And then-- OK, yeah.

00:37:45.430 --> 00:37:50.830 align:middle line:84%
This is just showing you where
the instrument comes from.

00:37:50.830 --> 00:37:53.770 align:middle line:84%
That is, it is true that
the cities that are small,

00:37:53.770 --> 00:37:55.870 align:middle line:84%
the cities of a few
hundred thousand,

00:37:55.870 --> 00:37:57.980 align:middle line:84%
tend to have only 10
or 15 radio stations.

00:37:57.980 --> 00:37:59.560 align:middle line:84%
And as markets
get bigger, you're

00:37:59.560 --> 00:38:00.860 align:middle line:90%
getting more radio stations.

00:38:00.860 --> 00:38:02.625 align:middle line:90%
And so that's the first stage.

00:38:02.625 --> 00:38:05.650 align:middle line:90%


00:38:05.650 --> 00:38:07.633 align:middle line:90%
And then the advertising prices.

00:38:07.633 --> 00:38:11.350 align:middle line:90%


00:38:11.350 --> 00:38:13.600 align:middle line:84%
As the listening
share increases--

00:38:13.600 --> 00:38:19.510 align:middle line:84%
this is per listener hour
advertising price or advertising

00:38:19.510 --> 00:38:20.470 align:middle line:90%
revenue--

00:38:20.470 --> 00:38:23.530 align:middle line:84%
advertising revenues
per listener hour

00:38:23.530 --> 00:38:29.687 align:middle line:84%
are clearly declining as the
listening share is going up.

00:38:29.687 --> 00:38:32.270 align:middle line:84%
And the model for this would be
there are some things that you

00:38:32.270 --> 00:38:33.870 align:middle line:90%
need to advertise on the radio.

00:38:33.870 --> 00:38:37.340 align:middle line:84%
And so if there's very few radio
commercial slots available,

00:38:37.340 --> 00:38:38.485 align:middle line:90%
we pay a lot for them.

00:38:38.485 --> 00:38:39.860 align:middle line:84%
And then beyond
that, you're just

00:38:39.860 --> 00:38:42.530 align:middle line:84%
left with playing radio
ads for things that you

00:38:42.530 --> 00:38:44.155 align:middle line:90%
could advertise anywhere else.

00:38:44.155 --> 00:38:45.530 align:middle line:84%
And the advertiser--
there's just

00:38:45.530 --> 00:38:47.630 align:middle line:84%
fewer advertisers who really
want those ads [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:47.630 --> 00:38:49.020 align:middle line:90%
the prices after the decline.

00:38:49.020 --> 00:38:55.100 align:middle line:90%


00:38:55.100 --> 00:38:56.310 align:middle line:90%
So what do they get?

00:38:56.310 --> 00:38:58.670 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, they estimate
sigma close to 1, entries

00:38:58.670 --> 00:39:00.000 align:middle line:90%
mostly business stealing.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:02.900 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, there's going
to be too much entry.

00:39:02.900 --> 00:39:07.840 align:middle line:84%
Almost shocking results here on
how much excessive entry there

00:39:07.840 --> 00:39:08.340 align:middle line:90%
is.

00:39:08.340 --> 00:39:14.150 align:middle line:84%
So welfare under free
entry is $5 billion.

00:39:14.150 --> 00:39:18.350 align:middle line:84%
A social planner could increase
social welfare by almost 40%

00:39:18.350 --> 00:39:20.870 align:middle line:84%
by restricting the
number of radio stations.

00:39:20.870 --> 00:39:25.040 align:middle line:84%
If we even had a monopolist--
so there was just one firm that

00:39:25.040 --> 00:39:27.133 align:middle line:84%
had a unique license
to operate all radio

00:39:27.133 --> 00:39:29.550 align:middle line:84%
stations in each market, and
that monopolist could operate

00:39:29.550 --> 00:39:31.860 align:middle line:84%
as many stations
as it wanted, it

00:39:31.860 --> 00:39:34.320 align:middle line:84%
would say we would do almost
as well under monopolist as we

00:39:34.320 --> 00:39:36.060 align:middle line:90%
do under the social planner.

00:39:36.060 --> 00:39:39.420 align:middle line:84%
So the estimates imply that we
want very few radio stations.

00:39:39.420 --> 00:39:41.170 align:middle line:84%
A monopolist wouldn't
open just one.

00:39:41.170 --> 00:39:46.140 align:middle line:84%
A monopolist would open
three or four or five

00:39:46.140 --> 00:39:47.200 align:middle line:90%
stations per market.

00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:49.530 align:middle line:84%
But in some sense,
the model is saying

00:39:49.530 --> 00:39:51.780 align:middle line:84%
that there's just an awful
lot of wasteful duplication

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:54.270 align:middle line:84%
of fixed costs, so we could
get by with many fewer radio

00:39:54.270 --> 00:39:56.220 align:middle line:90%
stations.

00:39:56.220 --> 00:40:03.720 align:middle line:84%
So in their data set, there are
2,509 within metro stations.

00:40:03.720 --> 00:40:06.870 align:middle line:84%
And they basically estimate
that we have four times as many

00:40:06.870 --> 00:40:14.850 align:middle line:84%
as we want, that optimal would
be to only have 649 stations.

00:40:14.850 --> 00:40:17.820 align:middle line:84%
What would happen in this world
where 3/4 of the radio stations

00:40:17.820 --> 00:40:19.680 align:middle line:90%
disappeared?

00:40:19.680 --> 00:40:23.920 align:middle line:84%
Advertising price would
go from 277 to 336.

00:40:23.920 --> 00:40:28.560 align:middle line:84%
So it goes up, but
only moderately.

00:40:28.560 --> 00:40:33.460 align:middle line:84%
Listening share would go from
12.9% of the population to 9.3%.

00:40:33.460 --> 00:40:37.800 align:middle line:84%
So it is true that more than a
quarter of all radio listening

00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:40.440 align:middle line:84%
would stop when all those
stations disappeared.

00:40:40.440 --> 00:40:43.590 align:middle line:84%
But the big social welfare
gain is the fixed costs

00:40:43.590 --> 00:40:47.590 align:middle line:84%
of operating all these radio
stations drops from $5 billion

00:40:47.590 --> 00:40:49.440 align:middle line:90%
to $1 billion.

00:40:49.440 --> 00:40:51.840 align:middle line:84%
So I just have to say,
this does make you

00:40:51.840 --> 00:40:53.340 align:middle line:90%
wonder a little bit about this.

00:40:53.340 --> 00:40:57.060 align:middle line:84%
Does this seem like some
Marvel superhero movie where

00:40:57.060 --> 00:40:58.530 align:middle line:84%
there's this evil
guy who's like,

00:40:58.530 --> 00:41:02.070 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to destroy 3/4
of all the people on Earth,

00:41:02.070 --> 00:41:04.200 align:middle line:84%
and then everyone's going
to be better off somehow.

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:08.730 align:middle line:84%
So these guys, they're very
well [INAUDIBLE] and destroy 3/4

00:41:08.730 --> 00:41:09.830 align:middle line:90%
of all the radio stations.

00:41:09.830 --> 00:41:11.580 align:middle line:84%
And yes, fewer people
are going to listen,

00:41:11.580 --> 00:41:13.290 align:middle line:84%
but we're going to
save all this money,

00:41:13.290 --> 00:41:16.140 align:middle line:84%
and we're all going
to be better off here.

00:41:16.140 --> 00:41:18.120 align:middle line:84%
One thing they note
is that it is true

00:41:18.120 --> 00:41:20.280 align:middle line:84%
that the advertising
listening share goes down

00:41:20.280 --> 00:41:22.520 align:middle line:84%
from 12.9% to 9.3%
Again, remember,

00:41:22.520 --> 00:41:24.270 align:middle line:84%
this is a calculation
where there are only

00:41:24.270 --> 00:41:26.830 align:middle line:84%
two types of people
in the world.

00:41:26.830 --> 00:41:30.540 align:middle line:84%
There are radio stations,
and there are advertisers.

00:41:30.540 --> 00:41:32.430 align:middle line:84%
And the people are
just intermediaries

00:41:32.430 --> 00:41:35.520 align:middle line:84%
who are just being used
to generate ad revenue.

00:41:35.520 --> 00:41:39.540 align:middle line:84%
They ask, what happened-- how
important would people have

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:43.140 align:middle line:84%
to be and people's enjoyment of
radio have to be in order to not

00:41:43.140 --> 00:41:45.460 align:middle line:84%
want to kill 3/4 of
the radio stations?

00:41:45.460 --> 00:41:48.700 align:middle line:84%
And my take on the number is
that it's really kind of small.

00:41:48.700 --> 00:41:52.373 align:middle line:84%
So the advertiser revenue
per listener hour,

00:41:52.373 --> 00:41:53.790 align:middle line:84%
if I'm reading the
data correctly,

00:41:53.790 --> 00:41:56.455 align:middle line:84%
is about 4.2 cents
per listener hour.

00:41:56.455 --> 00:41:58.830 align:middle line:84%
That is, if you think about
it, like you listen to radio,

00:41:58.830 --> 00:42:00.420 align:middle line:84%
how often you
actually buy anything

00:42:00.420 --> 00:42:01.690 align:middle line:90%
that you hear in a radio ad?

00:42:01.690 --> 00:42:03.690 align:middle line:84%
How much would someone
be willing to pay to have

00:42:03.690 --> 00:42:04.732 align:middle line:90%
you hear those radio ads?

00:42:04.732 --> 00:42:06.960 align:middle line:90%
The answer is almost nothing.

00:42:06.960 --> 00:42:11.040 align:middle line:84%
So to offset all these losses,
you would need consumer surplus

00:42:11.040 --> 00:42:16.290 align:middle line:84%
to be about 13 and 1/2 cents
per listener hour to say we

00:42:16.290 --> 00:42:18.780 align:middle line:84%
don't want to kill 3/4 of the
radio stations in the United

00:42:18.780 --> 00:42:19.300 align:middle line:90%
States.

00:42:19.300 --> 00:42:23.040 align:middle line:84%
And again, I-- again,
I don't know how much

00:42:23.040 --> 00:42:25.363 align:middle line:84%
people are willing to pay
for listening to the radio.

00:42:25.363 --> 00:42:28.030 align:middle line:84%
It is true that satellite radio
doesn't get that many customers.

00:42:28.030 --> 00:42:29.040 align:middle line:84%
And I don't know
how much Spotify

00:42:29.040 --> 00:42:30.210 align:middle line:90%
charges per listener hour.

00:42:30.210 --> 00:42:32.772 align:middle line:84%
But 13 and 1/2 cents is
not such a big number.

00:42:32.772 --> 00:42:34.230 align:middle line:84%
And so, in some
sense, you wouldn't

00:42:34.230 --> 00:42:36.630 align:middle line:84%
need customers to be
all that important

00:42:36.630 --> 00:42:39.130 align:middle line:84%
and not want to get rid of
all the firms in this way.

00:42:39.130 --> 00:42:42.610 align:middle line:90%


00:42:42.610 --> 00:42:45.700 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on that?

00:42:45.700 --> 00:42:47.710 align:middle line:84%
Oh, these are
just-- yeah, anyway.

00:42:47.710 --> 00:42:48.800 align:middle line:90%
Had the graphs up there.

00:42:48.800 --> 00:42:49.300 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:42:49.300 --> 00:42:51.700 align:middle line:84%
So the monopolist different
numbers-- so like, monopolist

00:42:51.700 --> 00:42:52.900 align:middle line:84%
is nearly as good
in their market.

00:42:52.900 --> 00:42:54.400 align:middle line:84%
The monopolist gets
all the way down

00:42:54.400 --> 00:42:58.130 align:middle line:84%
to just 341 radio
stations in 135 cities.

00:42:58.130 --> 00:43:01.540 align:middle line:84%
So we're going down literally
less than three radio stations

00:43:01.540 --> 00:43:02.830 align:middle line:90%
per city.

00:43:02.830 --> 00:43:06.100 align:middle line:84%
And again, the listening
share dropped substantially

00:43:06.100 --> 00:43:09.370 align:middle line:90%
under monopoly.

00:43:09.370 --> 00:43:12.670 align:middle line:84%
So some comments
I wanted to make.

00:43:12.670 --> 00:43:16.270 align:middle line:84%
In some sense, I didn't need
to actually do this paper--

00:43:16.270 --> 00:43:17.620 align:middle line:90%
do the empirics of this paper.

00:43:17.620 --> 00:43:21.880 align:middle line:84%
This is-- as I said, they model
this as a homogeneous good.

00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:23.920 align:middle line:84%
I already gave that
theorem that once when

00:43:23.920 --> 00:43:26.390 align:middle line:84%
you have a homogeneous good,
entry is always excessive.

00:43:26.390 --> 00:43:28.480 align:middle line:84%
So whatever parameters,
sigma and gamma

00:43:28.480 --> 00:43:31.300 align:middle line:84%
and whatever they've
estimated, we

00:43:31.300 --> 00:43:32.800 align:middle line:84%
know that this model
is going to say

00:43:32.800 --> 00:43:35.080 align:middle line:84%
there's too much entry
if the advertisers--

00:43:35.080 --> 00:43:39.915 align:middle line:84%
if the people are homogeneous
goods being sold to advertisers.

00:43:39.915 --> 00:43:41.290 align:middle line:84%
It's definitely
true that they're

00:43:41.290 --> 00:43:44.360 align:middle line:84%
estimating how excessive it
is by estimating that sigma

00:43:44.360 --> 00:43:44.870 align:middle line:90%
parameter.

00:43:44.870 --> 00:43:47.210 align:middle line:84%
And if that sigma parameter
had to not be zero,

00:43:47.210 --> 00:43:49.440 align:middle line:84%
they would have gotten a
much less excessive entry.

00:43:49.440 --> 00:43:53.225 align:middle line:84%
But the model is always
saying that entry is excessive

00:43:53.225 --> 00:43:55.100 align:middle line:84%
and doesn't have estimates
of the things that

00:43:55.100 --> 00:43:58.970 align:middle line:84%
will let you say there's
insufficient entry.

00:43:58.970 --> 00:44:00.480 align:middle line:90%
What would those things be?

00:44:00.480 --> 00:44:03.020 align:middle line:84%
For instance, if
you could estimate

00:44:03.020 --> 00:44:05.100 align:middle line:84%
how differentiated
are the goods--

00:44:05.100 --> 00:44:07.880 align:middle line:84%
are people who listen to
classical music versus top 40

00:44:07.880 --> 00:44:10.770 align:middle line:84%
music valued differently
by different advertisers?

00:44:10.770 --> 00:44:13.590 align:middle line:84%
Do the advertisers gain
from match quality?

00:44:13.590 --> 00:44:15.140 align:middle line:84%
That would be one
thing leading us

00:44:15.140 --> 00:44:17.665 align:middle line:84%
to want more stations
besides the consumer surplus.

00:44:17.665 --> 00:44:20.870 align:middle line:90%


00:44:20.870 --> 00:44:24.890 align:middle line:84%
The paper also is also
very limited in letting

00:44:24.890 --> 00:44:27.440 align:middle line:90%
quality vary across stations.

00:44:27.440 --> 00:44:30.290 align:middle line:84%
I think it is true that
there are big stations that

00:44:30.290 --> 00:44:31.590 align:middle line:90%
have very big fixed costs.

00:44:31.590 --> 00:44:33.410 align:middle line:84%
There are a lot of
college radio stations

00:44:33.410 --> 00:44:37.620 align:middle line:84%
that have very small fixed costs
and very low-powered antennas.

00:44:37.620 --> 00:44:42.200 align:middle line:84%
And you imagine that it's not
the case that we cut out 3/4

00:44:42.200 --> 00:44:44.430 align:middle line:84%
of stations, we cut out
3/4 of the fixed costs.

00:44:44.430 --> 00:44:47.097 align:middle line:84%
It may be that all those little
stations that no one's listening

00:44:47.097 --> 00:44:48.890 align:middle line:84%
to also are there
because they all

00:44:48.890 --> 00:44:52.790 align:middle line:84%
have very little fixed costs,
and we would like to do that.

00:44:52.790 --> 00:44:55.610 align:middle line:84%
The other thing that I think
they don't have the data to do,

00:44:55.610 --> 00:44:57.380 align:middle line:90%
but it would be--

00:44:57.380 --> 00:44:59.300 align:middle line:84%
I think a big question
in these media markets

00:44:59.300 --> 00:45:01.850 align:middle line:84%
is, what is the number of
advertising minutes per hour,

00:45:01.850 --> 00:45:04.820 align:middle line:84%
and how is that optimal
relative to consumer preferences

00:45:04.820 --> 00:45:06.480 align:middle line:90%
and advertiser preferences?

00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:12.740 align:middle line:84%
And it would be very
nice to have more on--

00:45:12.740 --> 00:45:16.310 align:middle line:84%
know more about how
do advertisers choose

00:45:16.310 --> 00:45:17.860 align:middle line:90%
the number of minutes per--

00:45:17.860 --> 00:45:20.360 align:middle line:84%
how do radio stations choose
the number of minutes per hour,

00:45:20.360 --> 00:45:23.010 align:middle line:84%
and what does the
trade-off on that look like

00:45:23.010 --> 00:45:26.330 align:middle line:84%
And you can imagine that we
don't get price variation

00:45:26.330 --> 00:45:29.060 align:middle line:84%
on the consumer side, but you
do get minutes of advertising

00:45:29.060 --> 00:45:30.480 align:middle line:90%
variation on the consumer side.

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:32.600 align:middle line:84%
And so you could
imagine that exploiting

00:45:32.600 --> 00:45:35.180 align:middle line:84%
that minute of
advertising variation

00:45:35.180 --> 00:45:41.690 align:middle line:84%
can be an interesting way to get
better at what are the-- what's

00:45:41.690 --> 00:45:44.080 align:middle line:84%
the consumer surplus of the
consumers who are listening

00:45:44.080 --> 00:45:45.560 align:middle line:90%
to the free radio?

00:45:45.560 --> 00:45:47.990 align:middle line:84%
And I should say there
are a number of papers

00:45:47.990 --> 00:45:50.360 align:middle line:84%
that do have advertising
data to try to address

00:45:50.360 --> 00:45:51.360 align:middle line:90%
some of these questions.

00:45:51.360 --> 00:45:52.985 align:middle line:84%
But I think it's
still an area where we

00:45:52.985 --> 00:45:55.940 align:middle line:90%
could do some interesting work.

00:45:55.940 --> 00:45:58.260 align:middle line:84%
So then the final
thing I want to do

00:45:58.260 --> 00:46:02.240 align:middle line:84%
is a couple of papers
that are, in some sense,

00:46:02.240 --> 00:46:05.210 align:middle line:84%
arguing that often,
these entry models are

00:46:05.210 --> 00:46:07.940 align:middle line:90%
doing the wrong model of entry.

00:46:07.940 --> 00:46:12.350 align:middle line:84%
Most of the models of entry that
I discussed in Tirole's textbook

00:46:12.350 --> 00:46:13.830 align:middle line:90%
are of this variety.

00:46:13.830 --> 00:46:17.330 align:middle line:84%
We have N for-- we have some
large population of firms,

00:46:17.330 --> 00:46:20.220 align:middle line:84%
and they're all choosing in
or out, paying a fixed cost.

00:46:20.220 --> 00:46:21.740 align:middle line:84%
And then once they
enter, there's

00:46:21.740 --> 00:46:24.378 align:middle line:84%
this symmetric
competition between them.

00:46:24.378 --> 00:46:25.670 align:middle line:90%
I think we know, though, that--

00:46:25.670 --> 00:46:27.410 align:middle line:84%
I've got a radio is an
example-- that it's not

00:46:27.410 --> 00:46:28.410 align:middle line:90%
symmetric competition.

00:46:28.410 --> 00:46:31.880 align:middle line:84%
There are some big firms, and
there are some small firms.

00:46:31.880 --> 00:46:37.640 align:middle line:84%
And what's in these next two
papers is thinking about,

00:46:37.640 --> 00:46:39.500 align:middle line:90%
even when you have products--

00:46:39.500 --> 00:46:41.702 align:middle line:84%
Like in radio, we think
there are high-quality,

00:46:41.702 --> 00:46:43.910 align:middle line:84%
or there's popular music
stations that lots of people

00:46:43.910 --> 00:46:44.410 align:middle line:90%
listen to.

00:46:44.410 --> 00:46:46.010 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
esoteric stations

00:46:46.010 --> 00:46:48.290 align:middle line:84%
that not a lot of people
listen to with low power.

00:46:48.290 --> 00:46:50.090 align:middle line:84%
Even when you look
at products that

00:46:50.090 --> 00:46:52.850 align:middle line:84%
seem like they should
be homogeneous,

00:46:52.850 --> 00:46:54.830 align:middle line:84%
it seems like symmetry
of competition

00:46:54.830 --> 00:46:56.765 align:middle line:90%
is not a correct answer.

00:46:56.765 --> 00:46:58.640 align:middle line:84%
And so these papers on
entry that are, again,

00:46:58.640 --> 00:47:00.182 align:middle line:84%
also papers really
about competition,

00:47:00.182 --> 00:47:03.650 align:middle line:84%
are arguing that this
model of entry and then

00:47:03.650 --> 00:47:05.270 align:middle line:84%
symmetric competition
seems like it

00:47:05.270 --> 00:47:06.750 align:middle line:90%
doesn't fit the data very well.

00:47:06.750 --> 00:47:09.830 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, here's an example
of homogeneous product--

00:47:09.830 --> 00:47:10.610 align:middle line:90%
mayonnaise.

00:47:10.610 --> 00:47:12.560 align:middle line:84%
I've heard that if you
do blind taste tests,

00:47:12.560 --> 00:47:15.240 align:middle line:84%
people have a very hard time
telling one brand of mayonnaise

00:47:15.240 --> 00:47:16.865 align:middle line:84%
apart from another
brand of mayonnaise.

00:47:16.865 --> 00:47:18.865 align:middle line:84%
Shouldn't mayonnaise just
be a homogeneous good,

00:47:18.865 --> 00:47:20.630 align:middle line:84%
where there's a
quantity of mayonnaise,

00:47:20.630 --> 00:47:22.130 align:middle line:84%
market-clearing
price in mayonnaise.

00:47:22.130 --> 00:47:24.422 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, it turns out empirically
that that's actually--

00:47:24.422 --> 00:47:25.100 align:middle line:90%
that's not true.

00:47:25.100 --> 00:47:27.170 align:middle line:84%
Well, it's not true that
mayonnaise doesn't all

00:47:27.170 --> 00:47:27.900 align:middle line:90%
taste the same.

00:47:27.900 --> 00:47:30.815 align:middle line:84%
It's not-- what's not true is
that people treat mayonnaise

00:47:30.815 --> 00:47:32.815 align:middle line:84%
like a homogeneous product
when they're deciding

00:47:32.815 --> 00:47:35.990 align:middle line:90%
what to buy in the supermarket.

00:47:35.990 --> 00:47:38.780 align:middle line:84%
So, in particular, one
of the things that's--

00:47:38.780 --> 00:47:41.400 align:middle line:90%


00:47:41.400 --> 00:47:44.040 align:middle line:84%
so there's this paper
by Golder and Tellis

00:47:44.040 --> 00:47:46.740 align:middle line:84%
that's a few decades
old now, noting

00:47:46.740 --> 00:47:49.890 align:middle line:84%
that there are many,
many leading brands that

00:47:49.890 --> 00:47:52.290 align:middle line:84%
have been leaders for
a very, very long time.

00:47:52.290 --> 00:47:54.480 align:middle line:84%
Like, when did Coca-Cola
become the leading cola

00:47:54.480 --> 00:47:55.840 align:middle line:90%
in the United States?

00:47:55.840 --> 00:47:58.050 align:middle line:84%
It's 100 and
something years ago.

00:47:58.050 --> 00:47:59.970 align:middle line:84%
And like many of
the products you can

00:47:59.970 --> 00:48:02.760 align:middle line:90%
think of have been the same--

00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:05.100 align:middle line:84%
the same leader has
been there for decades

00:48:05.100 --> 00:48:06.390 align:middle line:90%
and decades and decades.

00:48:06.390 --> 00:48:11.490 align:middle line:84%
And Eveready
batteries or whatever,

00:48:11.490 --> 00:48:13.500 align:middle line:90%
like the Pampers diapers.

00:48:13.500 --> 00:48:15.270 align:middle line:84%
These same firms just
persist for decade

00:48:15.270 --> 00:48:18.270 align:middle line:90%
after decade after decade.

00:48:18.270 --> 00:48:21.390 align:middle line:84%
The interesting fact that
Bronnenberg, Dhar, and Dubé were

00:48:21.390 --> 00:48:23.687 align:middle line:84%
bringing out in their paper
is that not only is it true

00:48:23.687 --> 00:48:25.895 align:middle line:84%
that in many consumer goods
there are market leaders,

00:48:25.895 --> 00:48:30.170 align:middle line:84%
but those market leaders
differ in similar markets.

00:48:30.170 --> 00:48:33.410 align:middle line:84%
So this is the mayonnaise
graph from their paper.

00:48:33.410 --> 00:48:38.390 align:middle line:84%
So there are two main mayonnaise
firms in the United States,

00:48:38.390 --> 00:48:40.860 align:middle line:84%
at least in the--
probably in the South.

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:43.780 align:middle line:84%
There's Kraft, and
there's Unilever.

00:48:43.780 --> 00:48:48.070 align:middle line:84%
And Kraft produces
Kraft mayonnaise.

00:48:48.070 --> 00:48:50.362 align:middle line:84%
Unilever produces
two brands, depending

00:48:50.362 --> 00:48:51.320 align:middle line:90%
on what city you're in.

00:48:51.320 --> 00:48:53.990 align:middle line:84%
They produce something called
Hellmann's Real mayonnaise.

00:48:53.990 --> 00:48:56.510 align:middle line:84%
They also produce something
called Best Foods mayonnaise.

00:48:56.510 --> 00:48:59.500 align:middle line:84%
You can tell from the jars
that Hellmann's and Best Foods

00:48:59.500 --> 00:49:00.830 align:middle line:90%
mayonnaises are--

00:49:00.830 --> 00:49:03.470 align:middle line:84%
they're the same mayonnaise
produced by Unilever.

00:49:03.470 --> 00:49:05.167 align:middle line:84%
Just in some
cities, they sell it

00:49:05.167 --> 00:49:07.000 align:middle line:84%
as Hellmann's, and some
cities, they sell it

00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:09.340 align:middle line:90%
as Best Foods mayonnaise.

00:49:09.340 --> 00:49:12.430 align:middle line:84%
But then what's striking
is this is data on--

00:49:12.430 --> 00:49:14.260 align:middle line:84%
over the course
of half a year, I

00:49:14.260 --> 00:49:17.290 align:middle line:84%
think-- on what's the
market share by week

00:49:17.290 --> 00:49:22.840 align:middle line:84%
of Kraft mayonnaise versus
Unilever mayonnaise in Denver?

00:49:22.840 --> 00:49:26.920 align:middle line:84%
And you can see in
Denver, 65%, 70% of people

00:49:26.920 --> 00:49:31.120 align:middle line:84%
buy Kraft mayonnaise,
and 15% to 20% of people

00:49:31.120 --> 00:49:33.490 align:middle line:90%
buy the Unilever mayonnaise.

00:49:33.490 --> 00:49:35.330 align:middle line:90%
And then you go to Los Angeles.

00:49:35.330 --> 00:49:37.930 align:middle line:84%
And in Los Angeles,
only 25% of the people

00:49:37.930 --> 00:49:41.680 align:middle line:84%
are buying Kraft
mayonnaise, and 60%,

00:49:41.680 --> 00:49:44.080 align:middle line:84%
I think it's the Best
Foods mayonnaise sold

00:49:44.080 --> 00:49:45.340 align:middle line:90%
on the West Coast.

00:49:45.340 --> 00:49:49.840 align:middle line:84%
And so it's striking that
like, in some sense, in Denver,

00:49:49.840 --> 00:49:52.270 align:middle line:84%
Kraft is the clear
mayonnaise leader.

00:49:52.270 --> 00:49:58.160 align:middle line:84%
And in Los Angeles, Unilever
is the clear mayonnaise leader.

00:49:58.160 --> 00:50:00.310 align:middle line:84%
And so there are these
different market leaders,

00:50:00.310 --> 00:50:02.350 align:middle line:84%
and they've been
different leaders.

00:50:02.350 --> 00:50:05.110 align:middle line:84%
And it seems quite stable
that there's a leading

00:50:05.110 --> 00:50:06.440 align:middle line:90%
mayonnaise that everyone buys.

00:50:06.440 --> 00:50:10.420 align:middle line:84%
And then there's a second
mayonnaise that some people buy.

00:50:10.420 --> 00:50:12.800 align:middle line:84%
They give other
examples in the paper.

00:50:12.800 --> 00:50:15.310 align:middle line:84%
If you look at ground
coffee, I think

00:50:15.310 --> 00:50:17.650 align:middle line:84%
of as another fairly
undifferentiated product.

00:50:17.650 --> 00:50:20.700 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if you
guys are wealthy enough

00:50:20.700 --> 00:50:23.670 align:middle line:84%
that you don't drink
frozen-ground coffee,

00:50:23.670 --> 00:50:25.350 align:middle line:84%
and you go to
Starbucks or whatever.

00:50:25.350 --> 00:50:27.880 align:middle line:84%
But you buy the frozen-ground
coffee in a can--

00:50:27.880 --> 00:50:30.090 align:middle line:84%
I don't know how different
the different cans are.

00:50:30.090 --> 00:50:33.690 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, Folgers' share
ranges from 16% in New York

00:50:33.690 --> 00:50:36.750 align:middle line:90%
to 59% in Des Moines, Iowa.

00:50:36.750 --> 00:50:39.480 align:middle line:84%
Maxwell House's share
is only 4% in Seattle,

00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:41.830 align:middle line:90%
but it's 46% in Pittsburgh.

00:50:41.830 --> 00:50:45.305 align:middle line:84%
And, again, these shares seem
to be persistent in the data.

00:50:45.305 --> 00:50:49.150 align:middle line:90%


00:50:49.150 --> 00:50:53.350 align:middle line:84%
So what Bronnenberg, Dhar,
and Dubé do to talk about this

00:50:53.350 --> 00:51:00.400 align:middle line:84%
is they just investigate this as
being a really, really long time

00:51:00.400 --> 00:51:04.540 align:middle line:84%
span echo of what happened when
these products were introduced.

00:51:04.540 --> 00:51:07.270 align:middle line:84%
And so their idea
is that in consumer

00:51:07.270 --> 00:51:11.230 align:middle line:84%
packaged goods
markets, people just--

00:51:11.230 --> 00:51:14.350 align:middle line:84%
somehow there's some model where
people learn to buy a brand.

00:51:14.350 --> 00:51:15.305 align:middle line:90%
They buy that brand.

00:51:15.305 --> 00:51:16.930 align:middle line:84%
And either they buy
that brand and they

00:51:16.930 --> 00:51:19.210 align:middle line:84%
get used to exactly what
that brand tastes like,

00:51:19.210 --> 00:51:21.620 align:middle line:84%
and that's what they think
mayonnaise should taste like.

00:51:21.620 --> 00:51:24.740 align:middle line:84%
Or they just know that
I buy Kraft mayonnaise.

00:51:24.740 --> 00:51:26.270 align:middle line:84%
My mother bought
Kraft mayonnaise.

00:51:26.270 --> 00:51:28.215 align:middle line:84%
Her mother bought Kraft
mayonnaise before her.

00:51:28.215 --> 00:51:29.590 align:middle line:84%
When you go to
mayonnaise, you've

00:51:29.590 --> 00:51:31.173 align:middle line:84%
seen your mother buy
Kraft mayonnaise.

00:51:31.173 --> 00:51:32.650 align:middle line:90%
You buy Kraft mayonnaise.

00:51:32.650 --> 00:51:35.500 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, that's the story
that they're thinking of,

00:51:35.500 --> 00:51:39.250 align:middle line:84%
is there's just something
about people forming tastes.

00:51:39.250 --> 00:51:42.515 align:middle line:84%
It could also be
something that's less--

00:51:42.515 --> 00:51:44.640 align:middle line:84%
it could also be something
that's less taste based.

00:51:44.640 --> 00:51:48.110 align:middle line:84%
It could also be there are
some advantages to dominance.

00:51:48.110 --> 00:51:50.270 align:middle line:84%
Like, if you're the
dominant mayonnaise,

00:51:50.270 --> 00:51:54.140 align:middle line:84%
you can go to the supermarket
and tell Albertsons,

00:51:54.140 --> 00:51:56.232 align:middle line:84%
I want my mayonnaise
at eye level.

00:51:56.232 --> 00:51:58.190 align:middle line:84%
I want you to put that
Unilever mayonnaise down

00:51:58.190 --> 00:51:59.360 align:middle line:90%
on the bottom shelf.

00:51:59.360 --> 00:52:01.130 align:middle line:84%
And that somehow the
dominant mayonnaise

00:52:01.130 --> 00:52:02.407 align:middle line:90%
gets put at eye level.

00:52:02.407 --> 00:52:04.490 align:middle line:84%
The second mayonnaise gets
put on the lower shelf.

00:52:04.490 --> 00:52:07.157 align:middle line:84%
And then people are just always
seeing the eye level mayonnaise,

00:52:07.157 --> 00:52:08.690 align:middle line:90%
and that's what they buy.

00:52:08.690 --> 00:52:09.890 align:middle line:90%
And so there's just some--

00:52:09.890 --> 00:52:15.170 align:middle line:84%
sometimes this is a story of
dominance producing dominance

00:52:15.170 --> 00:52:19.640 align:middle line:84%
and some kind of market power
being produced by large market

00:52:19.640 --> 00:52:20.930 align:middle line:90%
share.

00:52:20.930 --> 00:52:25.130 align:middle line:84%
But what they've done
is they go back to--

00:52:25.130 --> 00:52:26.880 align:middle line:90%
they go back 100 years.

00:52:26.880 --> 00:52:29.480 align:middle line:84%
And so all of these products,
like the Hellmann's mayonnaise

00:52:29.480 --> 00:52:35.000 align:middle line:84%
or the Kraft mayonnaise, they
find where those products were

00:52:35.000 --> 00:52:39.290 align:middle line:84%
first introduced in
the early 20th century,

00:52:39.290 --> 00:52:42.270 align:middle line:84%
and sometimes in
the 19th century.

00:52:42.270 --> 00:52:43.980 align:middle line:90%
And then they run--

00:52:43.980 --> 00:52:45.250 align:middle line:90%
they do two things.

00:52:45.250 --> 00:52:49.980 align:middle line:84%
So this is their main analysis
where they have 40 brands which

00:52:49.980 --> 00:52:52.620 align:middle line:90%
they could find these origins.

00:52:52.620 --> 00:52:56.010 align:middle line:84%
And they regress
the market share

00:52:56.010 --> 00:52:59.010 align:middle line:84%
that brand i is
getting in city m.

00:52:59.010 --> 00:53:01.440 align:middle line:90%
Forget about the c's.

00:53:01.440 --> 00:53:03.040 align:middle line:90%
It's a constant.

00:53:03.040 --> 00:53:06.180 align:middle line:84%
So Hellmann's is
better than Kraft.

00:53:06.180 --> 00:53:10.800 align:middle line:84%
Plus these coefficients
based on your distance

00:53:10.800 --> 00:53:12.010 align:middle line:90%
to the origin city.

00:53:12.010 --> 00:53:13.470 align:middle line:90%
Are you the origin city?

00:53:13.470 --> 00:53:15.190 align:middle line:84%
Are you 50 miles
from the origin city?

00:53:15.190 --> 00:53:19.060 align:middle line:84%
Are you 100 miles, 200
miles, 1,000 miles?

00:53:19.060 --> 00:53:21.000 align:middle line:84%
The thought being
that they don't

00:53:21.000 --> 00:53:23.160 align:middle line:84%
know when all these
mayonnaises got

00:53:23.160 --> 00:53:24.460 align:middle line:90%
to all these different places.

00:53:24.460 --> 00:53:28.536 align:middle line:84%
But their idea would
be that there's--

00:53:28.536 --> 00:53:32.160 align:middle line:90%
if we look at United States.

00:53:32.160 --> 00:53:35.160 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, I have a map
of the United States.

00:53:35.160 --> 00:53:37.650 align:middle line:84%
And then there's just
like Kraft mayonnaise gets

00:53:37.650 --> 00:53:40.642 align:middle line:90%
invented here in the year 1895.

00:53:40.642 --> 00:53:42.600 align:middle line:84%
And then their thought
is that it probably just

00:53:42.600 --> 00:53:43.720 align:middle line:90%
spread like this.

00:53:43.720 --> 00:53:45.880 align:middle line:90%
And so by 1910, it is here.

00:53:45.880 --> 00:53:47.880 align:middle line:90%
And by 1930, it was here.

00:53:47.880 --> 00:53:50.430 align:middle line:84%
And so we're seeing
the Kraft spreading,

00:53:50.430 --> 00:53:52.740 align:middle line:84%
and we're seeing
Hellmann's spreading.

00:53:52.740 --> 00:53:57.780 align:middle line:84%
And so how far you are
from where it started

00:53:57.780 --> 00:54:00.150 align:middle line:84%
is going to be how many
other mayonnaises were there

00:54:00.150 --> 00:54:00.970 align:middle line:90%
before you.

00:54:00.970 --> 00:54:02.987 align:middle line:84%
And so if you were
near the origin,

00:54:02.987 --> 00:54:05.070 align:middle line:84%
you were there and got to
establish the early lead

00:54:05.070 --> 00:54:06.270 align:middle line:90%
and now dominate.

00:54:06.270 --> 00:54:10.265 align:middle line:84%
If you're far from the origin,
by the time this mayonnaise gets

00:54:10.265 --> 00:54:11.640 align:middle line:84%
to California,
people were eating

00:54:11.640 --> 00:54:14.990 align:middle line:84%
several other mayonnaises,
and so it's stayed behind.

00:54:14.990 --> 00:54:18.060 align:middle line:90%


00:54:18.060 --> 00:54:19.920 align:middle line:84%
So that's the main
analysis because they

00:54:19.920 --> 00:54:21.733 align:middle line:84%
were able to do this
for 40 products.

00:54:21.733 --> 00:54:24.150 align:middle line:84%
The second thing they did is
there were six products where

00:54:24.150 --> 00:54:26.940 align:middle line:84%
they were actually
able to identify

00:54:26.940 --> 00:54:29.667 align:middle line:84%
at the city-by-city level,
when did Miller beer

00:54:29.667 --> 00:54:30.375 align:middle line:90%
get to Milwaukee?

00:54:30.375 --> 00:54:32.202 align:middle line:84%
And when did Miller
beer get to Chicago?

00:54:32.202 --> 00:54:33.910 align:middle line:84%
When did Miller beer
get to Indianapolis?

00:54:33.910 --> 00:54:35.560 align:middle line:90%
When did it get to Philadelphia?

00:54:35.560 --> 00:54:37.290 align:middle line:90%
When did it get to Miami?

00:54:37.290 --> 00:54:39.900 align:middle line:84%
So they actually were able to
track when each of the brands

00:54:39.900 --> 00:54:41.860 align:middle line:84%
entered all the
different cities.

00:54:41.860 --> 00:54:45.810 align:middle line:84%
And then they can regress
city-specific market shares

00:54:45.810 --> 00:54:48.250 align:middle line:90%
on dummies for entry order.

00:54:48.250 --> 00:54:50.370 align:middle line:84%
Were you there
before your rival?

00:54:50.370 --> 00:54:54.820 align:middle line:84%
Which mayonnaise got to Phoenix
first is the regression.

00:54:54.820 --> 00:54:58.680 align:middle line:90%


00:54:58.680 --> 00:54:59.180 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

00:54:59.180 --> 00:55:03.300 align:middle line:84%
So here's what their
data sample looks like.

00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:06.570 align:middle line:90%
So again, this is 40 products.

00:55:06.570 --> 00:55:08.270 align:middle line:84%
And so for each of
these 40 products--

00:55:08.270 --> 00:55:11.270 align:middle line:84%
let's go to mayonnaise
down at the bottom--

00:55:11.270 --> 00:55:12.880 align:middle line:90%
mayonnaise at the very bottom.

00:55:12.880 --> 00:55:15.620 align:middle line:84%
Kraft mayonnaise-- oh,
I was just about right.

00:55:15.620 --> 00:55:17.840 align:middle line:84%
Kraft mayonnaise started
in Salem, Illinois.

00:55:17.840 --> 00:55:21.020 align:middle line:84%
And it started Salem,
Illinois in 1931.

00:55:21.020 --> 00:55:23.510 align:middle line:84%
And Unilever mayonnaise,
which is Hellmann's

00:55:23.510 --> 00:55:24.750 align:middle line:90%
on the East Coast--

00:55:24.750 --> 00:55:29.270 align:middle line:84%
Hellmann's mayonnaise started in
New York City in the year 1905.

00:55:29.270 --> 00:55:31.550 align:middle line:84%
And so what they're
looking at is

00:55:31.550 --> 00:55:36.590 align:middle line:84%
how do how does the share
of Unilever versus Kraft

00:55:36.590 --> 00:55:41.140 align:middle line:84%
depend on your distance relative
to these two points of origin?

00:55:41.140 --> 00:55:46.490 align:middle line:90%


00:55:46.490 --> 00:55:46.990 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:55:46.990 --> 00:55:49.300 align:middle line:84%
So distance to the
origin analysis

00:55:49.300 --> 00:55:54.610 align:middle line:84%
finds that current market shares
are 18 percentage points higher

00:55:54.610 --> 00:55:57.310 align:middle line:84%
in the closest cities than
in the most distant cities.

00:55:57.310 --> 00:56:01.698 align:middle line:84%
So the mean share of these
products is like 22%.

00:56:01.698 --> 00:56:03.240 align:middle line:84%
So this is saying
that you get like--

00:56:03.240 --> 00:56:07.070 align:middle line:84%
you can think of the 18
difference as being--

00:56:07.070 --> 00:56:07.805 align:middle line:90%
you get 31%.

00:56:07.805 --> 00:56:12.630 align:middle line:90%


00:56:12.630 --> 00:56:13.130 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:56:13.130 --> 00:56:15.825 align:middle line:90%


00:56:15.825 --> 00:56:16.540 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:56:16.540 --> 00:56:19.600 align:middle line:84%
So you get like 31%
in the closest cities,

00:56:19.600 --> 00:56:24.040 align:middle line:84%
and you get 13% in the
farthest away cities.

00:56:24.040 --> 00:56:28.000 align:middle line:84%
So your market share is
just dramatically higher

00:56:28.000 --> 00:56:29.830 align:middle line:84%
in the cities near
where you started--

00:56:29.830 --> 00:56:32.500 align:middle line:84%
right where you started
than cities farther away

00:56:32.500 --> 00:56:33.550 align:middle line:90%
where you started.

00:56:33.550 --> 00:56:37.960 align:middle line:84%
We get this big effect
for your origin city.

00:56:37.960 --> 00:56:42.500 align:middle line:84%
And then it's declining as we
get to 250 miles, 500 miles,

00:56:42.500 --> 00:56:44.360 align:middle line:90%
1,000 miles.

00:56:44.360 --> 00:56:47.047 align:middle line:84%
It then seems to be fairly flat,
and then it goes down a lot

00:56:47.047 --> 00:56:49.380 align:middle line:84%
when you get to the opposite
coast of the United States.

00:56:49.380 --> 00:56:52.670 align:middle line:84%
So it's like East Coast
products are selling noticeably

00:56:52.670 --> 00:56:53.875 align:middle line:90%
less on the West Coast.

00:56:53.875 --> 00:56:55.250 align:middle line:84%
West Coast products
are selling--

00:56:55.250 --> 00:56:57.625 align:middle line:84%
or West Coast products are
selling notably-- considerably

00:56:57.625 --> 00:56:59.660 align:middle line:90%
less on the East Coast.

00:56:59.660 --> 00:57:02.990 align:middle line:84%
So you may think that
with our politics,

00:57:02.990 --> 00:57:06.143 align:middle line:84%
the coasts are actually
similar to each other,

00:57:06.143 --> 00:57:08.310 align:middle line:84%
and it's the middle of the
country that's different.

00:57:08.310 --> 00:57:10.610 align:middle line:84%
I think this graph is saying,
no, it's actually the--

00:57:10.610 --> 00:57:11.693 align:middle line:90%
it really is the distance.

00:57:11.693 --> 00:57:14.235 align:middle line:84%
It's like there are your East
Coast mayonnaises and your West

00:57:14.235 --> 00:57:15.050 align:middle line:90%
Coast mayonnaises.

00:57:15.050 --> 00:57:17.490 align:middle line:84%
There are East Coast bagels
and West Coast bagels.

00:57:17.490 --> 00:57:21.500 align:middle line:84%
And the market shares are most
different on the opposite coast

00:57:21.500 --> 00:57:24.510 align:middle line:90%
of the country.

00:57:24.510 --> 00:57:28.320 align:middle line:84%
And then the second
part of the analysis

00:57:28.320 --> 00:57:32.352 align:middle line:84%
about just is your market
share higher or low--

00:57:32.352 --> 00:57:33.810 align:middle line:84%
higher in the cities
that you reach

00:57:33.810 --> 00:57:37.500 align:middle line:84%
first versus second relative
to your main biggest rival?

00:57:37.500 --> 00:57:40.860 align:middle line:84%
And these numbers
are more moderate.

00:57:40.860 --> 00:57:42.260 align:middle line:90%
They range across products.

00:57:42.260 --> 00:57:45.240 align:middle line:84%
So like the Budweiser
versus Miller beer,

00:57:45.240 --> 00:57:49.350 align:middle line:84%
there's not that much difference
between Bud and Miller market

00:57:49.350 --> 00:57:52.140 align:middle line:90%
shares in the United States.

00:57:52.140 --> 00:57:54.480 align:middle line:84%
On average, whoever
got there first

00:57:54.480 --> 00:57:57.870 align:middle line:84%
is doing a 1.3
percentage point better

00:57:57.870 --> 00:58:00.330 align:middle line:90%
than whoever got there second.

00:58:00.330 --> 00:58:03.690 align:middle line:84%
In the mayonnaise example, who
got there first versus second

00:58:03.690 --> 00:58:06.500 align:middle line:90%
is 6.3 percentage points.

00:58:06.500 --> 00:58:14.694 align:middle line:90%


00:58:14.694 --> 00:58:15.690 align:middle line:90%
Sure.

00:58:15.690 --> 00:58:18.090 align:middle line:84%
So it is, I think,
fairly strong evidence,

00:58:18.090 --> 00:58:20.550 align:middle line:84%
especially that
first part of there

00:58:20.550 --> 00:58:21.970 align:middle line:90%
just are these asymmetries.

00:58:21.970 --> 00:58:24.130 align:middle line:84%
And remember, these are
events 100 years ago.

00:58:24.130 --> 00:58:28.530 align:middle line:84%
And so it does seem like there's
very, very long-lasting market

00:58:28.530 --> 00:58:30.400 align:middle line:90%
leadership advantages.

00:58:30.400 --> 00:58:34.187 align:middle line:84%
And if we're thinking
about entrance,

00:58:34.187 --> 00:58:36.270 align:middle line:84%
it's not like you enter,
and one year later you're

00:58:36.270 --> 00:58:37.437 align:middle line:90%
going to be on even footing.

00:58:37.437 --> 00:58:40.020 align:middle line:84%
It's like you're going to enter,
and 100 years later, you're

00:58:40.020 --> 00:58:43.500 align:middle line:84%
still going to have a
fraction of the share

00:58:43.500 --> 00:58:45.708 align:middle line:84%
that the leading firm had
100 years ago on something

00:58:45.708 --> 00:58:47.250 align:middle line:84%
like mayonnaise,
where it's clearly--

00:58:47.250 --> 00:58:48.900 align:middle line:84%
it's not like their mayonnaise
has just been better

00:58:48.900 --> 00:58:49.665 align:middle line:90%
over the years.

00:58:49.665 --> 00:58:53.600 align:middle line:90%


00:58:53.600 --> 00:58:54.100 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:58:54.100 --> 00:58:57.220 align:middle line:84%
So then the final paper I
was going to cover today--

00:58:57.220 --> 00:59:00.010 align:middle line:90%
Bronnenberg, Dubé, Gentzkow.

00:59:00.010 --> 00:59:03.520 align:middle line:84%
So this is really
digging into where

00:59:03.520 --> 00:59:06.280 align:middle line:84%
do long-lived
advantages come from?

00:59:06.280 --> 00:59:08.470 align:middle line:84%
They draw two main
distinctions for where--

00:59:08.470 --> 00:59:10.390 align:middle line:84%
I think two main
types of stories.

00:59:10.390 --> 00:59:12.850 align:middle line:84%
One is what they will
call demand side stories

00:59:12.850 --> 00:59:15.170 align:middle line:84%
or preference side--
preference-based stories.

00:59:15.170 --> 00:59:16.960 align:middle line:84%
Their preference-based
stories are

00:59:16.960 --> 00:59:20.620 align:middle line:84%
people develop
tastes for products

00:59:20.620 --> 00:59:22.540 align:middle line:90%
that they consume growing up.

00:59:22.540 --> 00:59:25.000 align:middle line:84%
Like, I'm just used
to this product.

00:59:25.000 --> 00:59:26.680 align:middle line:84%
I like the taste
of this product.

00:59:26.680 --> 00:59:29.050 align:middle line:84%
Or I've just learned
from my parents

00:59:29.050 --> 00:59:31.210 align:middle line:84%
that this is the
right product to buy.

00:59:31.210 --> 00:59:35.290 align:middle line:84%
I just-- I have preferences
about what this product is.

00:59:35.290 --> 00:59:37.210 align:middle line:84%
The supply side
stories are the ones

00:59:37.210 --> 00:59:42.040 align:middle line:84%
I talked about about things
like shelf space or advertising,

00:59:42.040 --> 00:59:45.460 align:middle line:84%
where if you're dominant, you
get the premier shelf space.

00:59:45.460 --> 00:59:48.920 align:middle line:84%
Or if you're dominant, it pays
you to put on TV commercials.

00:59:48.920 --> 00:59:50.780 align:middle line:84%
You put on TV commercials
that reinforce

00:59:50.780 --> 00:59:55.190 align:middle line:84%
the people's love for your
type-- for your product.

00:59:55.190 --> 00:59:59.450 align:middle line:84%
And so what Bronnenberg, Dubé,
and Gentzkow want to do is say,

00:59:59.450 --> 01:00:01.760 align:middle line:84%
does it look like
demand side, or does it

01:00:01.760 --> 01:00:03.860 align:middle line:90%
look like supply side?

01:00:03.860 --> 01:00:07.010 align:middle line:84%
And they have this sort of very
thoughtful idea, which is, how

01:00:07.010 --> 01:00:11.000 align:middle line:84%
about to investigate this, let's
look at people when they move.

01:00:11.000 --> 01:00:14.000 align:middle line:84%
And so like many of you
have moved to Boston.

01:00:14.000 --> 01:00:16.610 align:middle line:84%
And the question is, once
you've moved to Boston,

01:00:16.610 --> 01:00:20.505 align:middle line:84%
do you guys buy now the
Boston mayonnaise brand?

01:00:20.505 --> 01:00:22.130 align:middle line:84%
Or do you guys buy
the mayonnaise brand

01:00:22.130 --> 01:00:23.780 align:middle line:90%
that was big where you grew up?

01:00:23.780 --> 01:00:25.310 align:middle line:90%
So many of you probably--

01:00:25.310 --> 01:00:29.300 align:middle line:84%
I think-- I'd have to guess
that Boston is a Hellmann's

01:00:29.300 --> 01:00:32.990 align:middle line:84%
mayonnaise city, given
that, again, Hellmann's

01:00:32.990 --> 01:00:35.690 align:middle line:84%
started in New York, and
Kraft started in Illinois.

01:00:35.690 --> 01:00:38.330 align:middle line:84%
Again-- and there was a 6
percentage point difference.

01:00:38.330 --> 01:00:40.970 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, I would guess
that Hellmann's is the Boston

01:00:40.970 --> 01:00:41.490 align:middle line:90%
mayonnaise.

01:00:41.490 --> 01:00:44.360 align:middle line:84%
And the question is, do you
guys, when you move here--

01:00:44.360 --> 01:00:45.230 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

01:00:45.230 --> 01:00:47.660 align:middle line:84%
I guess not that many of you
are from the Midwest either.

01:00:47.660 --> 01:00:50.570 align:middle line:84%
But when you guys
moved here, did you

01:00:50.570 --> 01:00:52.010 align:middle line:84%
start buying
Hellmann's mayonnaise

01:00:52.010 --> 01:00:53.390 align:middle line:84%
and give up your
Kraft mayonnaise?

01:00:53.390 --> 01:00:55.598 align:middle line:84%
Or do you continue to buy
the Kraft mayonnaise, which

01:00:55.598 --> 01:00:57.980 align:middle line:84%
is totally available in
Star Market and Whole Foods

01:00:57.980 --> 01:00:58.620 align:middle line:90%
and whatever.

01:00:58.620 --> 01:01:02.240 align:middle line:84%
Well, I'm not sure if
it's in Whole Foods.

01:01:02.240 --> 01:01:04.222 align:middle line:90%
But anyway, that's their idea.

01:01:04.222 --> 01:01:06.180 align:middle line:84%
And you might think,
well, that's a great idea.

01:01:06.180 --> 01:01:08.310 align:middle line:90%
How would I ever find this out?

01:01:08.310 --> 01:01:12.287 align:middle line:84%
And this is where, again,
you get the recognition

01:01:12.287 --> 01:01:14.120 align:middle line:84%
of the importance in
our profession of being

01:01:14.120 --> 01:01:17.180 align:middle line:84%
very well-funded and
well-connected sometimes.

01:01:17.180 --> 01:01:23.210 align:middle line:84%
So what-- University of Chicago
has a long-standing relationship

01:01:23.210 --> 01:01:24.630 align:middle line:90%
with Nielsen's.

01:01:24.630 --> 01:01:27.470 align:middle line:90%


01:01:27.470 --> 01:01:29.300 align:middle line:84%
Firms like Nielsen,
besides doing

01:01:29.300 --> 01:01:30.710 align:middle line:84%
things for people they
have relationships with,

01:01:30.710 --> 01:01:32.127 align:middle line:84%
also do things for
people who have

01:01:32.127 --> 01:01:33.540 align:middle line:90%
money to pay them to do things.

01:01:33.540 --> 01:01:36.440 align:middle line:84%
And so what Bronnenberg,
Dubé, and Gentzkow

01:01:36.440 --> 01:01:39.110 align:middle line:90%
got Nielsen to do is--

01:01:39.110 --> 01:01:41.300 align:middle line:84%
have I talked about
Nielsen Homescan Panel?

01:01:41.300 --> 01:01:42.170 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:01:42.170 --> 01:01:43.970 align:middle line:84%
Nielsen Homescan
Panel-- if you want it--

01:01:43.970 --> 01:01:46.910 align:middle line:84%
it's actually-- a lot of this
data is available for free

01:01:46.910 --> 01:01:50.870 align:middle line:84%
through the University
of Chicago website.

01:01:50.870 --> 01:01:52.460 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, Nielsen
Homescan Panel

01:01:52.460 --> 01:01:55.040 align:middle line:84%
is they've got a panel
of thousands of people

01:01:55.040 --> 01:01:56.465 align:middle line:90%
across the United States.

01:01:56.465 --> 01:01:58.340 align:middle line:84%
And what these thousands
of people do is they

01:01:58.340 --> 01:02:00.200 align:middle line:90%
all have a part-time job.

01:02:00.200 --> 01:02:04.130 align:middle line:84%
Their part-time job is recording
everything that they buy,

01:02:04.130 --> 01:02:06.470 align:middle line:84%
and they're employed
by Nielsen to do that.

01:02:06.470 --> 01:02:09.582 align:middle line:84%
And so they're given like
a supermarket scanner.

01:02:09.582 --> 01:02:10.790 align:middle line:90%
So you go to the supermarket.

01:02:10.790 --> 01:02:11.418 align:middle line:90%
You buy stuff.

01:02:11.418 --> 01:02:13.460 align:middle line:84%
You scan it all in the
scanner when you're there.

01:02:13.460 --> 01:02:16.043 align:middle line:84%
And then you bring it home, and
as you take it out of the bag,

01:02:16.043 --> 01:02:17.900 align:middle line:90%
you scan it again.

01:02:17.900 --> 01:02:20.330 align:middle line:84%
And you just scan it in so
that Nielsen knows everything

01:02:20.330 --> 01:02:21.230 align:middle line:90%
that you bought.

01:02:21.230 --> 01:02:23.490 align:middle line:84%
And you're supposed to do
this everywhere you go.

01:02:23.490 --> 01:02:25.940 align:middle line:84%
You go to Target, you
come back, you scan it.

01:02:25.940 --> 01:02:26.887 align:middle line:90%
You go to the CVS.

01:02:26.887 --> 01:02:27.470 align:middle line:90%
You come back.

01:02:27.470 --> 01:02:28.010 align:middle line:90%
You scan it.

01:02:28.010 --> 01:02:28.970 align:middle line:90%
You go to the supermarket.

01:02:28.970 --> 01:02:29.553 align:middle line:90%
You come back.

01:02:29.553 --> 01:02:30.150 align:middle line:90%
You scan it.

01:02:30.150 --> 01:02:33.440 align:middle line:84%
You basically just
are home scans.

01:02:33.440 --> 01:02:35.360 align:middle line:84%
What these people do
is they buy stuff,

01:02:35.360 --> 01:02:37.470 align:middle line:84%
and then they just record
everything they got.

01:02:37.470 --> 01:02:40.290 align:middle line:84%
And then they're also
recording where they got it.

01:02:40.290 --> 01:02:44.150 align:middle line:84%
And so because Nielsen is
already in the Star Market,

01:02:44.150 --> 01:02:47.360 align:middle line:84%
and Nielsen is already in the
Whole Foods, you bring it home.

01:02:47.360 --> 01:02:48.020 align:middle line:90%
You scan it.

01:02:48.020 --> 01:02:49.610 align:middle line:84%
They know what you paid
for it because they

01:02:49.610 --> 01:02:50.870 align:middle line:84%
know where you bought
it, and they know what

01:02:50.870 --> 01:02:52.470 align:middle line:90%
that store is charging for it.

01:02:52.470 --> 01:02:56.570 align:middle line:84%
So you get this sort
of data set of what

01:02:56.570 --> 01:03:01.250 align:middle line:84%
is it that people at the
individual level are purchasing.

01:03:01.250 --> 01:03:05.630 align:middle line:84%
And you can also get aggregate
market shares out of this.

01:03:05.630 --> 01:03:08.980 align:middle line:84%
Nielsen Homescan Panel
did not tell them--

01:03:08.980 --> 01:03:10.730 align:middle line:84%
it tells you where the
people are located,

01:03:10.730 --> 01:03:12.000 align:middle line:90%
that this person is in Boston.

01:03:12.000 --> 01:03:16.160 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't tell you that
they grew up in Illinois

01:03:16.160 --> 01:03:19.670 align:middle line:84%
and moved to Boston when they
went to college at age of 19.

01:03:19.670 --> 01:03:22.220 align:middle line:84%
So what they got
Nielsen to do is

01:03:22.220 --> 01:03:25.070 align:middle line:84%
survey the members of the
Homescan Panel and ask them.

01:03:25.070 --> 01:03:28.710 align:middle line:90%
So what state were you born in?

01:03:28.710 --> 01:03:31.910 align:middle line:84%
When did you leave
your state of birth?

01:03:31.910 --> 01:03:38.300 align:middle line:84%
When do you-- how long have you
been in your current location?

01:03:38.300 --> 01:03:40.910 align:middle line:84%
And ask you other questions
like, what's your gender,

01:03:40.910 --> 01:03:44.923 align:middle line:84%
or what's your highest level of
education completed, and so on.

01:03:44.923 --> 01:03:46.340 align:middle line:84%
Normally, you would
think you send

01:03:46.340 --> 01:03:47.900 align:middle line:84%
a survey like that to
people, it's very hard

01:03:47.900 --> 01:03:48.930 align:middle line:90%
to get them to respond.

01:03:48.930 --> 01:03:51.300 align:middle line:84%
But since this
survey was sponsored

01:03:51.300 --> 01:03:54.870 align:middle line:84%
by Homescan, who is an employer
of all the Homescan people,

01:03:54.870 --> 01:03:58.800 align:middle line:84%
they got a 65% response
rate on their survey

01:03:58.800 --> 01:03:59.993 align:middle line:90%
asking this information.

01:03:59.993 --> 01:04:02.160 align:middle line:84%
They're able to merge that
information together with

01:04:02.160 --> 01:04:03.990 align:middle line:84%
the people's
purchasing patterns,

01:04:03.990 --> 01:04:08.400 align:middle line:84%
and they get this remarkable
data set of 80,000 individuals

01:04:08.400 --> 01:04:11.100 align:middle line:90%
living in 49,000 households.

01:04:11.100 --> 01:04:13.770 align:middle line:84%
And they know their
life history of moving,

01:04:13.770 --> 01:04:17.172 align:middle line:84%
and then they can examine
what those people purchased.

01:04:17.172 --> 01:04:20.720 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's an interesting
contrast with Bresnahan

01:04:20.720 --> 01:04:22.255 align:middle line:90%
and Reiss, where we have like--

01:04:22.255 --> 01:04:23.630 align:middle line:84%
we're opening up
the phone books,

01:04:23.630 --> 01:04:25.963 align:middle line:84%
and we're counting there are
three dentists or whatever,

01:04:25.963 --> 01:04:29.690 align:middle line:84%
and that's for 200 different
cities versus this 80,000

01:04:29.690 --> 01:04:32.900 align:middle line:84%
people, you've got basically
their shopping lives laid out

01:04:32.900 --> 01:04:35.335 align:middle line:84%
in front of you, as well as
some of their life history.

01:04:35.335 --> 01:04:40.760 align:middle line:90%


01:04:40.760 --> 01:04:47.150 align:middle line:84%
Nielsen classifies UPC codes
by product category and brand.

01:04:47.150 --> 01:04:52.200 align:middle line:84%
In this paper, they've
picked 238 different modules.

01:04:52.200 --> 01:04:54.470 align:middle line:90%
So a module could be mayonnaise.

01:04:54.470 --> 01:04:56.980 align:middle line:90%
So they pick 238 modules.

01:04:56.980 --> 01:05:00.650 align:middle line:84%
They pick modules where they
have at least 5,000 households

01:05:00.650 --> 01:05:05.030 align:middle line:84%
purchasing that item and where
the top two brands are owned

01:05:05.030 --> 01:05:06.600 align:middle line:90%
by different firms.

01:05:06.600 --> 01:05:09.560 align:middle line:84%
So, for instance, in
the cream cheese module,

01:05:09.560 --> 01:05:12.420 align:middle line:84%
the number one cream cheese
is Philadelphia cream cheese.

01:05:12.420 --> 01:05:15.210 align:middle line:84%
The number two cream cheese is
Philadelphia Light cream cheese.

01:05:15.210 --> 01:05:17.080 align:middle line:84%
So they would throw
out cream cheese.

01:05:17.080 --> 01:05:18.930 align:middle line:84%
But in mayonnaise,
number one mayonnaise

01:05:18.930 --> 01:05:20.640 align:middle line:90%
is Unilever mayonnaise.

01:05:20.640 --> 01:05:22.570 align:middle line:84%
Number two mayonnaise
is Kraft mayonnaise.

01:05:22.570 --> 01:05:26.300 align:middle line:90%
They keep mayonnaise.

01:05:26.300 --> 01:05:28.010 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, the current
market share data

01:05:28.010 --> 01:05:31.160 align:middle line:84%
is also supplemented
with historical data

01:05:31.160 --> 01:05:33.740 align:middle line:84%
at the state level that's
obtained from some published

01:05:33.740 --> 01:05:36.860 align:middle line:84%
surveys that asked people
about, what brand of mayonnaise

01:05:36.860 --> 01:05:37.830 align:middle line:90%
do you normally buy?

01:05:37.830 --> 01:05:41.210 align:middle line:84%
And they found these archival
things from the '40s up

01:05:41.210 --> 01:05:42.920 align:middle line:90%
to the '60s.

01:05:42.920 --> 01:05:45.230 align:middle line:84%
I won't talk so much
about that part, though.

01:05:45.230 --> 01:05:47.315 align:middle line:84%
Actually, they also
do advertising data.

01:05:47.315 --> 01:05:49.190 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to talk
so much about what they

01:05:49.190 --> 01:05:51.710 align:middle line:90%
do with the advertising data.

01:05:51.710 --> 01:05:54.420 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, so here is
part of their product list.

01:05:54.420 --> 01:05:57.500 align:middle line:84%
And, again, it's
238 things long.

01:05:57.500 --> 01:06:01.603 align:middle line:90%
But so all of these are--

01:06:01.603 --> 01:06:02.270 align:middle line:90%
well, let's see.

01:06:02.270 --> 01:06:03.895 align:middle line:84%
Down here at the
bottom, I've got beer.

01:06:03.895 --> 01:06:05.420 align:middle line:90%
I've talked about beer before.

01:06:05.420 --> 01:06:09.740 align:middle line:84%
So the number one
beer is Budweiser.

01:06:09.740 --> 01:06:13.100 align:middle line:90%
The number two beer is Miller.

01:06:13.100 --> 01:06:16.790 align:middle line:84%
What they do is they record
the aggregate purchase share.

01:06:16.790 --> 01:06:21.200 align:middle line:84%
So aggregate share here
means share of product one.

01:06:21.200 --> 01:06:24.540 align:middle line:84%
Aggregate purchase share
is share of Budweiser

01:06:24.540 --> 01:06:27.280 align:middle line:84%
and the fraction people
buying Budweiser or Miller.

01:06:27.280 --> 01:06:31.830 align:middle line:84%
So 64% of the people who buy
Budweiser or Miller buy Miller.

01:06:31.830 --> 01:06:35.860 align:middle line:84%
And then, again, these are the
cross-state standard deviation.

01:06:35.860 --> 01:06:37.163 align:middle line:90%
So it's 64 on average.

01:06:37.163 --> 01:06:38.830 align:middle line:84%
But in different
states, it's different.

01:06:38.830 --> 01:06:43.470 align:middle line:84%
So it's 64 plus or minus 19
for Budweiser versus Miller.

01:06:43.470 --> 01:06:48.180 align:middle line:84%
Some of the products,
like of bread,

01:06:48.180 --> 01:06:50.950 align:middle line:84%
Nature's Own versus Sara
Lee Soft and Smooth,

01:06:50.950 --> 01:06:54.276 align:middle line:84%
it's like 50/50 between
those two plus or minus 32.

01:06:54.276 --> 01:06:59.160 align:middle line:90%


01:06:59.160 --> 01:07:01.480 align:middle line:84%
Some of them there's
very little variation.

01:07:01.480 --> 01:07:03.900 align:middle line:84%
So antacids--
Prilosec and Rolaids

01:07:03.900 --> 01:07:06.030 align:middle line:90%
are the top two antacids.

01:07:06.030 --> 01:07:10.050 align:middle line:84%
Prilosec is a 71% share
in that comparison.

01:07:10.050 --> 01:07:11.790 align:middle line:84%
It's a more effective
drug, I guess.

01:07:11.790 --> 01:07:14.040 align:middle line:90%
And it's 71 plus or minus 8.

01:07:14.040 --> 01:07:19.370 align:middle line:90%


01:07:19.370 --> 01:07:20.770 align:middle line:84%
I look at some of
these, and they

01:07:20.770 --> 01:07:23.530 align:middle line:84%
seem like things that are
very similar products.

01:07:23.530 --> 01:07:27.220 align:middle line:84%
Like, I think I would be shocked
if Comet abrasive cleaning

01:07:27.220 --> 01:07:29.440 align:middle line:84%
powder and Ajax
abrasive cleaning powder

01:07:29.440 --> 01:07:30.500 align:middle line:90%
are very different.

01:07:30.500 --> 01:07:32.150 align:middle line:84%
Some of them, like
the bakery products,

01:07:32.150 --> 01:07:34.110 align:middle line:84%
maybe it seems like
they're not as homogeneous

01:07:34.110 --> 01:07:35.760 align:middle line:90%
as some of the other things.

01:07:35.760 --> 01:07:40.820 align:middle line:90%


01:07:40.820 --> 01:07:43.830 align:middle line:84%
So what does
shopper's homescan--

01:07:43.830 --> 01:07:46.590 align:middle line:84%
or these homescan shoppers in
the United States look like?

01:07:46.590 --> 01:07:49.590 align:middle line:84%
I don't know how this compares
to the US population as a whole.

01:07:49.590 --> 01:07:51.560 align:middle line:90%
Someone must know that.

01:07:51.560 --> 01:07:58.460 align:middle line:84%
About 27% of the households
in their sample are migrants.

01:07:58.460 --> 01:08:00.808 align:middle line:84%
I guess, obviously,
almost everybody

01:08:00.808 --> 01:08:02.600 align:middle line:84%
I know is a migrant,
which I guess tells me

01:08:02.600 --> 01:08:03.920 align:middle line:90%
that migrants are unusual--

01:08:03.920 --> 01:08:07.850 align:middle line:84%
that we're somewhat unusual
relative to the US population.

01:08:07.850 --> 01:08:12.260 align:middle line:84%
But 27% of the households
were born in a different state

01:08:12.260 --> 01:08:13.910 align:middle line:90%
where they now reside.

01:08:13.910 --> 01:08:17.270 align:middle line:84%
16% were born in a different
census region-- so conditional

01:08:17.270 --> 01:08:19.010 align:middle line:90%
on having moved states.

01:08:19.010 --> 01:08:23.000 align:middle line:84%
Many people have
moved fairly far.

01:08:23.000 --> 01:08:27.703 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, this is-- you'll
notice people don't really

01:08:27.703 --> 01:08:28.620 align:middle line:90%
move to the Northeast.

01:08:28.620 --> 01:08:29.995 align:middle line:84%
People are born
in the Northeast,

01:08:29.995 --> 01:08:31.529 align:middle line:84%
and then they stay
in the Northeast.

01:08:31.529 --> 01:08:33.290 align:middle line:84%
Very few people,
especially from the West,

01:08:33.290 --> 01:08:36.740 align:middle line:84%
move to the Northeast,
given the weather.

01:08:36.740 --> 01:08:42.090 align:middle line:84%
More people in the South move
to the South from the Northeast

01:08:42.090 --> 01:08:43.020 align:middle line:90%
or from the Midwest.

01:08:43.020 --> 01:08:45.870 align:middle line:90%


01:08:45.870 --> 01:08:50.700 align:middle line:84%
Ages-- the people in their
sample tend to be in their 40s,

01:08:50.700 --> 01:08:52.590 align:middle line:90%
50s, 60s.

01:08:52.590 --> 01:08:56.490 align:middle line:84%
You get very few people in their
20s who want to have a scanner

01:08:56.490 --> 01:09:00.600 align:middle line:84%
and scan everything they
buy at the grocery store.

01:09:00.600 --> 01:09:02.100 align:middle line:84%
Age leaving state
of birth-- there's

01:09:02.100 --> 01:09:03.675 align:middle line:84%
an awful lot of
variation in that.

01:09:03.675 --> 01:09:06.300 align:middle line:84%
You get a lot of people who left
before they were 10 years old,

01:09:06.300 --> 01:09:10.350 align:middle line:84%
a lot of people who move in
their 20s, not surprisingly.

01:09:10.350 --> 01:09:14.760 align:middle line:84%
And then you do get people who
also move in their 60s when

01:09:14.760 --> 01:09:16.960 align:middle line:90%
they're retiring.

01:09:16.960 --> 01:09:19.279 align:middle line:90%
So what's the main analysis?

01:09:19.279 --> 01:09:22.229 align:middle line:84%
The main thing is to ask,
let's look at these people

01:09:22.229 --> 01:09:26.399 align:middle line:84%
and ask when they move, how far
do they move from their birth

01:09:26.399 --> 01:09:31.170 align:middle line:84%
place purchasing patterns to
their destination purchasing

01:09:31.170 --> 01:09:32.189 align:middle line:90%
patterns?

01:09:32.189 --> 01:09:36.420 align:middle line:84%
And so, they write
mu sj for the share

01:09:36.420 --> 01:09:40.380 align:middle line:84%
of people who never move-- the
share of nonmigrants in state

01:09:40.380 --> 01:09:42.810 align:middle line:90%
s who buy product j.

01:09:42.810 --> 01:09:46.020 align:middle line:84%
And then mu s prime j
is the people in state

01:09:46.020 --> 01:09:47.930 align:middle line:90%
s prime who never move--

01:09:47.930 --> 01:09:50.470 align:middle line:84%
what fraction of
them buy product j?

01:09:50.470 --> 01:09:52.470 align:middle line:84%
And so what I'm doing is
I'm thinking of someone

01:09:52.470 --> 01:09:56.940 align:middle line:84%
who moves from state s to
state s prime and saying,

01:09:56.940 --> 01:10:00.810 align:middle line:84%
how does their
purchasing compare to--

01:10:00.810 --> 01:10:05.710 align:middle line:84%
how far in the direction of s
prime has their purchasing--

01:10:05.710 --> 01:10:09.090 align:middle line:84%
how far along the way from
mu s to mu s prime has

01:10:09.090 --> 01:10:13.710 align:middle line:84%
their purchasing moved, assuming
they started at mu sj when they

01:10:13.710 --> 01:10:14.730 align:middle line:90%
were in--

01:10:14.730 --> 01:10:18.450 align:middle line:84%
before they moved 20 years
ago when I don't observe them?

01:10:18.450 --> 01:10:20.970 align:middle line:90%
So the question is, if this--

01:10:20.970 --> 01:10:23.370 align:middle line:90%
if you still purchase--

01:10:23.370 --> 01:10:29.110 align:middle line:84%
if these migrants still purchase
exactly what they purchased--

01:10:29.110 --> 01:10:31.257 align:middle line:84%
well, if they were
representative of the population

01:10:31.257 --> 01:10:33.840 align:middle line:84%
in state s, and still purchase
what they purchased before they

01:10:33.840 --> 01:10:36.810 align:middle line:90%
left, beta would be 0.

01:10:36.810 --> 01:10:40.650 align:middle line:84%
If they all purchase are
exactly representative

01:10:40.650 --> 01:10:43.300 align:middle line:84%
of the nonmigrants in
their new home and purchase

01:10:43.300 --> 01:10:47.070 align:middle line:84%
what people in the new home
purchase, beta would be 1.

01:10:47.070 --> 01:10:49.620 align:middle line:84%
So beta is like, what
fraction of the way

01:10:49.620 --> 01:10:53.760 align:middle line:84%
have people moved from looking
like where they were born people

01:10:53.760 --> 01:10:56.670 align:middle line:90%
to where they now live people?

01:10:56.670 --> 01:11:00.510 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to basically
have a regression estimating

01:11:00.510 --> 01:11:08.940 align:middle line:84%
beta ij as a function of the
age that you were when you moved

01:11:08.940 --> 01:11:11.912 align:middle line:84%
and the time that you've
been in your new location.

01:11:11.912 --> 01:11:14.370 align:middle line:84%
So you might think if you moved
when you were one year old,

01:11:14.370 --> 01:11:16.420 align:middle line:84%
you've been your
location for 60 years,

01:11:16.420 --> 01:11:19.178 align:middle line:84%
you ought to look like people
who grew up in that location

01:11:19.178 --> 01:11:20.970 align:middle line:84%
where you moved when
you were one year old,

01:11:20.970 --> 01:11:22.637 align:middle line:84%
other than maybe your
parents taught you

01:11:22.637 --> 01:11:24.630 align:middle line:90%
about what mayonnaise to buy.

01:11:24.630 --> 01:11:28.680 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, if you're 60 years
old, and you moved at age--

01:11:28.680 --> 01:11:31.230 align:middle line:84%
if you moved at age 60,
and you've been there

01:11:31.230 --> 01:11:33.950 align:middle line:84%
for six months, you might
expect that those people would

01:11:33.950 --> 01:11:35.700 align:middle line:84%
look more like the
origin people, at least

01:11:35.700 --> 01:11:37.188 align:middle line:90%
on the taste side.

01:11:37.188 --> 01:11:40.370 align:middle line:90%


01:11:40.370 --> 01:11:42.620 align:middle line:84%
And then they do this via
weighted least squares.

01:11:42.620 --> 01:11:44.120 align:middle line:84%
Because if you think
about it like--

01:11:44.120 --> 01:11:47.150 align:middle line:84%
if mu s and mu s prime are
almost identical-- we're

01:11:47.150 --> 01:11:49.230 align:middle line:84%
getting almost no
information from that market,

01:11:49.230 --> 01:11:51.047 align:middle line:84%
and it's where mu
s and mu s prime

01:11:51.047 --> 01:11:53.130 align:middle line:84%
are very different that
we're getting information,

01:11:53.130 --> 01:11:55.220 align:middle line:84%
and so they do a weighted
least squares estimate

01:11:55.220 --> 01:11:59.090 align:middle line:84%
weighting the behavior
of people who made moves

01:11:59.090 --> 01:12:01.957 align:middle line:90%
to more different places.

01:12:01.957 --> 01:12:04.040 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so they've got a
few figures that they do.

01:12:04.040 --> 01:12:05.340 align:middle line:90%
They've got this one.

01:12:05.340 --> 01:12:09.440 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes they're estimating
this coefficient separately.

01:12:09.440 --> 01:12:11.030 align:middle line:90%
Again, you've got 80,000 people.

01:12:11.030 --> 01:12:13.880 align:middle line:84%
You've got a lot of
people who move at age 15

01:12:13.880 --> 01:12:16.050 align:middle line:84%
and have been
there for 35 years.

01:12:16.050 --> 01:12:18.260 align:middle line:84%
So they have
separate coefficients

01:12:18.260 --> 01:12:25.850 align:middle line:84%
for every age at move and
every year since move.

01:12:25.850 --> 01:12:28.710 align:middle line:84%
I find this one hard to see,
though, in three dimensions.

01:12:28.710 --> 01:12:32.040 align:middle line:84%
It's hard to see how high these
things are versus the slope.

01:12:32.040 --> 01:12:35.190 align:middle line:84%
So easier to read
is these ones here.

01:12:35.190 --> 01:12:39.030 align:middle line:84%
They just go-- they collapse
it down to one dimension.

01:12:39.030 --> 01:12:43.320 align:middle line:84%
And what this is saying
is that an awful lot of it

01:12:43.320 --> 01:12:45.660 align:middle line:90%
seems to be supply side.

01:12:45.660 --> 01:12:49.140 align:middle line:84%
Because if you look at
people who have just moved--

01:12:49.140 --> 01:12:53.040 align:middle line:84%
so it's zero to four
years since their move,

01:12:53.040 --> 01:12:57.600 align:middle line:84%
they're immediately like
60-something percent of the way

01:12:57.600 --> 01:13:00.570 align:middle line:90%
to the new purchasing patterns.

01:13:00.570 --> 01:13:03.300 align:middle line:84%
When you look at people who
moved 40 or 50 years ago,

01:13:03.300 --> 01:13:06.960 align:middle line:84%
they are like 80% or 90% of the
way to the purchasing patterns.

01:13:06.960 --> 01:13:10.470 align:middle line:84%
So it seems like there's a
big supply side element that

01:13:10.470 --> 01:13:13.320 align:middle line:84%
makes you immediately on
moving here to Boston,

01:13:13.320 --> 01:13:15.660 align:middle line:84%
you start buying
Hellman's mayonnaise.

01:13:15.660 --> 01:13:18.340 align:middle line:84%
But there is something
that's longer term.

01:13:18.340 --> 01:13:21.390 align:middle line:84%
And so this could be
that advertising playing

01:13:21.390 --> 01:13:25.900 align:middle line:84%
on you over 40 years, or this
could just be your tastes adapt.

01:13:25.900 --> 01:13:26.942 align:middle line:90%
You go to friends houses.

01:13:26.942 --> 01:13:28.317 align:middle line:84%
They serve Hellmann's
mayonnaise.

01:13:28.317 --> 01:13:30.010 align:middle line:84%
You think, wow, that
Hellmann's is good.

01:13:30.010 --> 01:13:31.920 align:middle line:90%
I should buy that myself.

01:13:31.920 --> 01:13:35.550 align:middle line:84%
So there's adaptation
over time, but a lot of it

01:13:35.550 --> 01:13:39.360 align:middle line:90%
happens on day one.

01:13:39.360 --> 01:13:41.850 align:middle line:84%
And then here's the
age at move graph.

01:13:41.850 --> 01:13:44.607 align:middle line:84%
The people who moved when they
were children-- obviously,

01:13:44.607 --> 01:13:46.440 align:middle line:84%
the people who moved
when they were children

01:13:46.440 --> 01:13:50.357 align:middle line:84%
have already had to
have lived for quite

01:13:50.357 --> 01:13:52.440 align:middle line:84%
a while in the new location
because they were only

01:13:52.440 --> 01:13:53.620 align:middle line:90%
surveying adults.

01:13:53.620 --> 01:13:56.430 align:middle line:84%
But the people who
moved at children--

01:13:56.430 --> 01:13:57.570 align:middle line:90%
you moved at age one.

01:13:57.570 --> 01:13:59.960 align:middle line:84%
You do retain something
of your birth mayonnaise.

01:13:59.960 --> 01:14:02.830 align:middle line:84%
So this is you're getting
something about mayonnaise

01:14:02.830 --> 01:14:04.120 align:middle line:90%
from your parents.

01:14:04.120 --> 01:14:07.600 align:middle line:84%
Because it can't be that you
moved at age one, therefore,

01:14:07.600 --> 01:14:13.000 align:middle line:84%
you remember the mayonnaise
of your babyhood.

01:14:13.000 --> 01:14:14.750 align:middle line:84%
So there is something
that you're getting.

01:14:14.750 --> 01:14:19.870 align:middle line:84%
It's only 0.9, but then it
declines in the age it moves.

01:14:19.870 --> 01:14:24.970 align:middle line:84%
But it stays that even people
who moved in their 60s,

01:14:24.970 --> 01:14:27.640 align:middle line:84%
they're mostly adjusting
to the new mayonnaise.

01:14:27.640 --> 01:14:33.920 align:middle line:90%


01:14:33.920 --> 01:14:34.420 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:14:34.420 --> 01:14:35.590 align:middle line:90%
So what do I have here?

01:14:35.590 --> 01:14:37.370 align:middle line:90%
What does it say?

01:14:37.370 --> 01:14:40.090 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, people instantly
move 60% of the way.

01:14:40.090 --> 01:14:45.880 align:middle line:84%
This suggests that it is a
lot of supply side stuff.

01:14:45.880 --> 01:14:47.170 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:14:47.170 --> 01:14:49.040 align:middle line:84%
People become more
similar over time.

01:14:49.040 --> 01:14:50.290 align:middle line:90%
I said that.

01:14:50.290 --> 01:14:52.590 align:middle line:84%
Betas are related
to age and move.

01:14:52.590 --> 01:14:56.440 align:middle line:84%
So age 60-plus, you're still
getting a big movement toward

01:14:56.440 --> 01:14:58.985 align:middle line:90%
new things.

01:14:58.985 --> 01:15:01.310 align:middle line:84%
Oh, number 4-- data
on premove migrants.

01:15:01.310 --> 01:15:03.250 align:middle line:84%
Again, there's this
thing of you guys

01:15:03.250 --> 01:15:08.800 align:middle line:84%
moved to Boston because you're
not the typical people where

01:15:08.800 --> 01:15:09.820 align:middle line:90%
you grew up.

01:15:09.820 --> 01:15:11.680 align:middle line:84%
You probably don't
drink the same coffee

01:15:11.680 --> 01:15:15.710 align:middle line:84%
that people where
you grew up drink.

01:15:15.710 --> 01:15:17.380 align:middle line:84%
You probably don't
drink the same beer

01:15:17.380 --> 01:15:19.420 align:middle line:84%
that people where
you grew up drink.

01:15:19.420 --> 01:15:21.962 align:middle line:84%
You probably don't-- maybe you
don't have the same mayonnaise

01:15:21.962 --> 01:15:23.650 align:middle line:90%
like people you grew up.

01:15:23.650 --> 01:15:25.960 align:middle line:84%
So the question is,
is this in part--

01:15:25.960 --> 01:15:30.455 align:middle line:84%
is this like 60% figure
because the people--

01:15:30.455 --> 01:15:32.080 align:middle line:84%
remember, most of
the analysis are only

01:15:32.080 --> 01:15:33.730 align:middle line:84%
observing where
people are today.

01:15:33.730 --> 01:15:36.010 align:middle line:84%
So the 60% figure is that
people living in Boston,

01:15:36.010 --> 01:15:38.422 align:middle line:90%
maybe it's that like--

01:15:38.422 --> 01:15:39.630 align:middle line:90%
it's like, you are different.

01:15:39.630 --> 01:15:41.330 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, you get your
coffee at Starbucks.

01:15:41.330 --> 01:15:43.040 align:middle line:84%
Whereas, people you
grew up with didn't

01:15:43.040 --> 01:15:44.780 align:middle line:84%
go to Starbucks or
something like that.

01:15:44.780 --> 01:15:47.690 align:middle line:84%
And you moved to Boston because
you were like the Starbucks type

01:15:47.690 --> 01:15:49.820 align:middle line:90%
person or something.

01:15:49.820 --> 01:15:51.050 align:middle line:90%
They have evidence, though--

01:15:51.050 --> 01:15:54.620 align:middle line:84%
they actually look at
the month-by-month data.

01:15:54.620 --> 01:15:57.140 align:middle line:84%
They don't-- can't track you
precisely, but they're ask--

01:15:57.140 --> 01:16:02.510 align:middle line:84%
they did their survey
in October of 2007.

01:16:02.510 --> 01:16:05.120 align:middle line:90%
And then-- no, sorry.

01:16:05.120 --> 01:16:07.640 align:middle line:84%
They did their survey
in September of 2008.

01:16:07.640 --> 01:16:08.605 align:middle line:90%
And they asked people--

01:16:08.605 --> 01:16:09.980 align:middle line:84%
like, they asked
people, how long

01:16:09.980 --> 01:16:11.150 align:middle line:84%
have you been in
your current state?

01:16:11.150 --> 01:16:11.850 align:middle line:90%
And what are the answers?

01:16:11.850 --> 01:16:12.892 align:middle line:90%
The answers are integers.

01:16:12.892 --> 01:16:16.810 align:middle line:84%
So it's less than 1, 1
to 2, 2 to 3, whatever.

01:16:16.810 --> 01:16:18.770 align:middle line:84%
So all the people
who said less than--

01:16:18.770 --> 01:16:21.440 align:middle line:84%
I'm living in some
state in September 2008.

01:16:21.440 --> 01:16:23.240 align:middle line:90%
I've been here less than a year.

01:16:23.240 --> 01:16:27.440 align:middle line:84%
They know that they moved
sometime between October of 2007

01:16:27.440 --> 01:16:29.010 align:middle line:90%
and September of 2008.

01:16:29.010 --> 01:16:31.100 align:middle line:84%
They didn't ask for
the month of the move.

01:16:31.100 --> 01:16:32.570 align:middle line:84%
But what they can
then do is say,

01:16:32.570 --> 01:16:35.960 align:middle line:84%
look at these people who moved
here within the past year,

01:16:35.960 --> 01:16:39.920 align:middle line:84%
and look at what mayonnaise
did they eat before they moved.

01:16:39.920 --> 01:16:42.560 align:middle line:84%
And what they find
is that before they

01:16:42.560 --> 01:16:47.660 align:middle line:84%
moved over this year,
it's looking to me

01:16:47.660 --> 01:16:50.240 align:middle line:84%
like the people were
only like 10% of the way

01:16:50.240 --> 01:16:51.660 align:middle line:90%
to their new destination.

01:16:51.660 --> 01:16:53.330 align:middle line:90%
So maybe they were more worldly.

01:16:53.330 --> 01:16:56.570 align:middle line:84%
Maybe they already had
more Boston kind of tastes.

01:16:56.570 --> 01:17:00.380 align:middle line:84%
But they're only maybe
10% Boston taste people.

01:17:00.380 --> 01:17:03.290 align:middle line:84%
And then you look at them as
they move through the year.

01:17:03.290 --> 01:17:05.120 align:middle line:90%
And by the time they moved--

01:17:05.120 --> 01:17:08.680 align:middle line:84%
like that 60%, 1 to 4 years
isn't 60% 0 to 4 years.

01:17:08.680 --> 01:17:13.442 align:middle line:84%
That's 60% the within the first
12 months after you get there.

01:17:13.442 --> 01:17:15.650 align:middle line:84%
So some of these people have
only been here one month

01:17:15.650 --> 01:17:17.630 align:middle line:84%
and are already buying
the new mayonnaise.

01:17:17.630 --> 01:17:19.650 align:middle line:84%
Some of them have been
here six or nine months.

01:17:19.650 --> 01:17:23.870 align:middle line:84%
But it seems that
this 65% thing looks

01:17:23.870 --> 01:17:25.860 align:middle line:90%
like a causal effect of moving.

01:17:25.860 --> 01:17:26.930 align:middle line:90%
And it looks like--

01:17:26.930 --> 01:17:29.790 align:middle line:84%
or maybe 80% of it's a
causal effect of moving.

01:17:29.790 --> 01:17:31.550 align:middle line:84%
Some part of it is
like a preexisting,

01:17:31.550 --> 01:17:33.330 align:middle line:90%
underlying heterogeneity.

01:17:33.330 --> 01:17:35.393 align:middle line:84%
But then there's
this big, big one.

01:17:35.393 --> 01:17:41.320 align:middle line:90%


01:17:41.320 --> 01:17:43.300 align:middle line:90%
And, again, it's just--

01:17:43.300 --> 01:17:46.360 align:middle line:84%
they're just--
80,000 is a good--

01:17:46.360 --> 01:17:48.680 align:middle line:84%
80,000 data points
is a good thing.

01:17:48.680 --> 01:17:52.660 align:middle line:84%
And 80,000 data points times
238 products is a good thing.

01:17:52.660 --> 01:17:56.207 align:middle line:84%
And just-- this is
vantage of data scale,

01:17:56.207 --> 01:17:58.790 align:middle line:84%
as you can do these things that
would be very, very hard to do

01:17:58.790 --> 01:18:00.772 align:middle line:90%
with any small [INAUDIBLE].

01:18:00.772 --> 01:18:06.370 align:middle line:90%


01:18:06.370 --> 01:18:09.180 align:middle line:90%
Questions on that?

01:18:09.180 --> 01:18:12.110 align:middle line:90%
So that is what I got today.

01:18:12.110 --> 01:18:18.100 align:middle line:84%
So next week, again, is theory
Monday, empirics Wednesday.

01:18:18.100 --> 01:18:21.400 align:middle line:84%
Next week, the theory topic
is strategic investment.

01:18:21.400 --> 01:18:24.910 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is going to
be a very Tirole textbook

01:18:24.910 --> 01:18:26.430 align:middle line:90%
kind of lecture on Monday.

01:18:26.430 --> 01:18:29.140 align:middle line:84%
If you look at chapter
8 of the Tirole's book.

01:18:29.140 --> 01:18:30.770 align:middle line:84%
It's the longest
chapter in the book.

01:18:30.770 --> 01:18:33.190 align:middle line:84%
So I will just try
to go over the basics

01:18:33.190 --> 01:18:34.320 align:middle line:90%
of strategic investment.

01:18:34.320 --> 01:18:36.220 align:middle line:84%
And strategic investment
is about thinking

01:18:36.220 --> 01:18:38.650 align:middle line:84%
of things firms do to
affect entry in markets.

01:18:38.650 --> 01:18:41.860 align:middle line:84%
Because if entry really is the
primary determinant of markups,

01:18:41.860 --> 01:18:45.130 align:middle line:84%
really should want to affect
entry and keep firms out

01:18:45.130 --> 01:18:46.622 align:middle line:90%
of markets [INAUDIBLE].

01:18:46.622 --> 01:18:49.270 align:middle line:90%


01:18:49.270 --> 01:18:51.150 align:middle line:90%
See you on Monday.

01:18:51.150 --> 01:19:03.000 align:middle line:90%